-- CA_0376: Georgia gun-policy Season 3 reasoning, text only — annex labels to words, unsourced quotes to paraphrase.
--
-- The Georgia batch (write/ga-01, approved 2026-11-12) was replayed through the coding-time text checks of PR #1405
-- (backend/scripts/lib/stage5Ingest.ts checkAtIngest). On the LIVE text of the 148 seated rows (blanks store no
-- reasoning) it found two defect kinds; the bill-name and not-in-passage defects in the replay were in the coder files
-- only, already removed at write time or on rows that are blanks, so none is live.
--   1. 150 annex or ruling labels (R5-AZ, R5-L, H12, C2, C37) on 100 rows. Voters read this text, and the gate reads
--      S1-S8 as Senate bills. Nearly every label sat beside the rule already in words, so it is dropped; where it did
--      not, the rule is written out ("it takes silencers off a ban whose only exception is federal registration";
--      "its bar on a gun-owner registry where none exists repeals nothing"; "SB 67 (a bar on enforcing federal gun
--      rules) is adjacent").
--   2. 20 quotes on 18 rows taken from a finding the row does not cite, so the voter sees quote marks with no source.
--      Each is paraphrased without quote marks. Citing the finding instead would change the sources, which this
--      text-only fix does not touch. No seat rests on any of them, since none is a cited source.
-- The fixed text passes all four checks (148 of 148); a planted control of each kind went red first.
--
-- Reasoning text only. value, sources, evidence, review rows and outcomes are unchanged; the post-verify asserts every
-- value. Each row is guarded on its exact old text (kept below, so the overwritten value stays on record): the file is
-- idempotent and refuses a row someone else has since edited. STAGE5.md "Reasoning text is voter-facing".

CREATE TEMP TABLE _ca0376_fix (politician_id uuid PRIMARY KEY, value int NOT NULL, old_text text NOT NULL, new_text text NOT NULL);

INSERT INTO _ca0376_fix (politician_id, value, old_text, new_text) VALUES
  ('02633f01-08f0-4011-a2d1-7a1a63b1b01f', 5,
   $q$Yea on GA HB 1324 (House Vote #665, 2026-03-06, 87-76, single-subject, not gated), which repeals the silencer definition and strikes 'or silencer' from O.C.G.A. 16-11-122 ('No person shall have in his or her possession any sawed-off shotgun, sawed-off rifle, machine gun, or dangerous weapon'), a ban whose exception in 16-11-124 is registration 'in accordance with the dictates of the National Firearms Act'. Under R5-AZ that is removing an accessory from a registration law, the rung 'Repeal laws that require a permit or registration for a gun'. His Yea on HB 1018 (registry ban, R5-L) shows the loosening side only; HB 1023 is gated (151-11) and SB 204 is multi-subject preemption; no restriction act is on file.$q$,
   $q$Yea on GA HB 1324 (House Vote #665, 2026-03-06, 87-76, single-subject, not gated), which repeals the silencer definition and strikes 'or silencer' from O.C.G.A. 16-11-122 ('No person shall have in his or her possession any sawed-off shotgun, sawed-off rifle, machine gun, or dangerous weapon'), a ban whose exception in 16-11-124 is registration 'in accordance with the dictates of the National Firearms Act'. That is removing an accessory from a registration law, the rung 'Repeal laws that require a permit or registration for a gun'. His Yea on HB 1018 (registry ban) shows the loosening side only; HB 1023 is gated (151-11) and SB 204 is multi-subject preemption; no restriction act is on file.$q$),
  ('02e6e5fd-b554-4411-bbe6-e274b24a35a9', 5,
   $q$He cast a Yea on SB 319 (2022 House Vote #817, 100-67; the same roll call is filed twice in the store and is counted once), whose operative text says 'no person shall carry a weapon unless he or she is a lawful weapons carrier' and defines a lawful weapons carrier as anyone 'licensed or eligible for a license', so the permit to carry is no longer required. That is rung 5: 'Repeal laws that require a permit or registration for a gun, such as the permit to carry'. He also cast a Yea on HB 1324 (2026 House Vote #665, 87-76), which strikes 'or silencer' from O.C.G.A. 16-11-122's dangerous-weapons ban, whose exception (16-11-124) is NFA registration, i.e. it takes an accessory off a registration law (annex R5-AZ). His Yeas on HB 218, HB 280, HB 292, HB 859 and SB 259, and his sponsorship of HB 859 (campus carry), are rung 4 acts on the same side, so the furthest act governs. HB 1018 is direction-only, HB 1023 is gated, and SB 204 is multi-subject. His 2014 words about supporting the right to bear arms state no rung.$q$,
   $q$He cast a Yea on SB 319 (2022 House Vote #817, 100-67; the same roll call is filed twice in the store and is counted once), whose operative text says 'no person shall carry a weapon unless he or she is a lawful weapons carrier' and defines a lawful weapons carrier as anyone 'licensed or eligible for a license', so the permit to carry is no longer required. That is rung 5: 'Repeal laws that require a permit or registration for a gun, such as the permit to carry'. He also cast a Yea on HB 1324 (2026 House Vote #665, 87-76), which strikes 'or silencer' from O.C.G.A. 16-11-122's dangerous-weapons ban, whose exception (16-11-124) is NFA registration, i.e. it takes an accessory off a registration law. His Yeas on HB 218, HB 280, HB 292, HB 859 and SB 259, and his sponsorship of HB 859 (campus carry), are rung 4 acts on the same side, so the furthest act governs. HB 1018 is direction-only, HB 1023 is gated, and SB 204 is multi-subject. His 2014 words about supporting the right to bear arms state no rung.$q$),
  ('05a68843-680e-45a9-8218-97dab0381324', 5,
   $q$Rung 5, "Repeal laws that require a permit or registration for a gun, such as the permit to carry": a Yea on SB 319 (2021-22; House Vote #817, 2022-03-30, 100-67; single-subject, not gated), whose operative text defines a "lawful weapons carrier" as "any person who is licensed or eligible for a license" and provides "no person shall carry a weapon unless he or she is a lawful weapons carrier", so a carry licence is no longer required (the store holds this vote twice, as GA-SB-319-2021 and GA-SB-319-2022; it is counted once), and a Yea on HB 1324 (2025-26; House Vote #665, 2026-03-06, 87-76; single-subject, not gated), which strikes "silencer" from O.C.G.A. 16-11-122 ("No person shall have in his or her possession any sawed-off shotgun, sawed-off rifle, machine gun, or dangerous weapon" … "except as provided in Code Section 16-11-124"), a ban whose exception is items registered "in accordance with the dictates of the National Firearms Act": removing an accessory from a registration law (R5-AZ). Further Yeas on rung 4 carry bills (HB 218 reciprocity, SB 259, campus carry HB 859 and HB 280, HB 292) are on the same side and sit under the furthest act. HB 1018 (2023-24) is a merchant-code rule (adjacent) plus a bar on a gun-owner registry where none exists (direction-only, R5-L); HB 1023 (weapon detection at school entrances, "weapon" wider than firearms) is a school-safety measure (adjacent) and is gated near-unanimous (151-11); SB 204 (2025) is multi-subject and its gun part is preemption (adjacent). No rung 1-3 act is on file. His 2014 House-release quote ("protect and preserve the Second Amendment") names no policy (rhetorical).$q$,
   $q$Rung 5, "Repeal laws that require a permit or registration for a gun, such as the permit to carry": a Yea on SB 319 (2021-22; House Vote #817, 2022-03-30, 100-67; single-subject, not gated), whose operative text defines a "lawful weapons carrier" as "any person who is licensed or eligible for a license" and provides "no person shall carry a weapon unless he or she is a lawful weapons carrier", so a carry licence is no longer required (the store holds this vote twice, as GA-SB-319-2021 and GA-SB-319-2022; it is counted once), and a Yea on HB 1324 (2025-26; House Vote #665, 2026-03-06, 87-76; single-subject, not gated), which strikes "silencer" from O.C.G.A. 16-11-122 ("No person shall have in his or her possession any sawed-off shotgun, sawed-off rifle, machine gun, or dangerous weapon" … "except as provided in Code Section 16-11-124"), a ban whose exception is items registered "in accordance with the dictates of the National Firearms Act": removing an accessory from a registration law. Further Yeas on rung 4 carry bills (HB 218 reciprocity, SB 259, campus carry HB 859 and HB 280, HB 292) are on the same side and sit under the furthest act. HB 1018 (2023-24) is a merchant-code rule (adjacent) plus a bar on a gun-owner registry where none exists (direction-only); HB 1023 (weapon detection at school entrances, "weapon" wider than firearms) is a school-safety measure (adjacent) and is gated near-unanimous (151-11); SB 204 (2025) is multi-subject and its gun part is preemption (adjacent). No rung 1-3 act is on file. His 2014 House release, which speaks of protecting the Second Amendment, names no policy (rhetorical).$q$),
  ('0761f7c4-67ba-4f38-9ea0-cd22c17b9065', 5,
   $q$He is a listed sponsor of SB 319 and voted Yea on its Senate agree vote, 2022-04-01. SB 319 (2021-2022, the Constitutional Carry Act; stored twice as GA-SB-319-2021 and GA-SB-319-2022, counted once) is single-subject and passed 100-67 in the House and 34-22 on the Senate agree vote (not gated). Its operative text makes the carrier a "lawful weapons carrier", meaning anyone "licensed or eligible for a license", and provides "no person shall carry a weapon unless he or she is a lawful weapons carrier", so the carry licence is no longer required: rung 5, "Repeal laws that require a permit or registration for a gun, such as the permit to carry". He also sponsored and voted Yea (30-20) on SB 499 (2026), silencers struck from the ban whose only exception is NFA registration (R5-AZ, rung 5). In his own statement of 2024-01-25 he says no permit should be needed to carry a weapon in Georgia, backing the permitless carry law in force. Rung 4 votes (HB 218, SB 259) are on the same side; SB 67 is closure C2 (adjacent). No rung 1-3 act.$q$,
   $q$He is a listed sponsor of SB 319 and voted Yea on its Senate agree vote, 2022-04-01. SB 319 (2021-2022, the Constitutional Carry Act; stored twice as GA-SB-319-2021 and GA-SB-319-2022, counted once) is single-subject and passed 100-67 in the House and 34-22 on the Senate agree vote (not gated). Its operative text makes the carrier a "lawful weapons carrier", meaning anyone "licensed or eligible for a license", and provides "no person shall carry a weapon unless he or she is a lawful weapons carrier", so the carry licence is no longer required: rung 5, "Repeal laws that require a permit or registration for a gun, such as the permit to carry". He also sponsored and voted Yea (30-20) on SB 499 (2026), silencers struck from the ban whose only exception is NFA registration (rung 5). In his own statement of 2024-01-25 he says no permit should be needed to carry a weapon in Georgia, backing the permitless carry law in force. Rung 4 votes (HB 218, SB 259) are on the same side; SB 67 (a bar on enforcing federal gun rules) is adjacent. No rung 1-3 act.$q$),
  ('07f1594a-b9ac-409e-b074-fc65c9316c8e', 3,
   $q$He is a listed sponsor (LegiScan) of three single-subject 2025 Senate bills with no floor vote on file (not gated): SB 92, the Red Flag Protective Order Act ("the court shall issue a risk protection order" against a respondent who "poses a significant danger"); SB 66, barring people with family-violence convictions or protective orders from guns and ordering them to "surrender all firearms" … "within 24 hours"; and SB 19, a ten-day waiting period for semiautomatic assault weapons (a waiting period, not a type ban). Each is rung 3, "Add limits on buying, having or carrying a gun, such as" … "red-flag orders, waiting periods"; none bans a type. His Senate Nays on SB 319, SB 499 and the carry bills agree in direction. SR 605 (2025) is a resolution with no force of law and no recorded vote; it states no gun rule, so it is read as rhetorical and neither seats nor conflicts. His 2026-01-13 words that localities should oversee gun "purchase, ownership, storage and sale" are about which level decides (adjacent, H12).$q$,
   $q$He is a listed sponsor (LegiScan) of three single-subject 2025 Senate bills with no floor vote on file (not gated): SB 92, the Red Flag Protective Order Act ("the court shall issue a risk protection order" against a respondent who "poses a significant danger"); SB 66, barring people with family-violence convictions or protective orders from guns and ordering them to "surrender all firearms" … "within 24 hours"; and SB 19, a ten-day waiting period for semiautomatic assault weapons (a waiting period, not a type ban). Each is rung 3, "Add limits on buying, having or carrying a gun, such as" … "red-flag orders, waiting periods"; none bans a type. His Senate Nays on SB 319, SB 499 and the carry bills agree in direction. SR 605 (2025) is a resolution with no force of law and no recorded vote; it states no gun rule, so it is read as rhetorical and neither seats nor conflicts. His 2026-01-13 words that localities should oversee the buying, owning, storing and selling of guns are about which level decides (adjacent).$q$),
  ('085cfb33-a561-4bdf-bc8f-6a74d5e85cd1', 5,
   $q$Yea on the House passage of SB 319 (2022-03-30, counted once) and Yea on HB 1324 (2026-03-06). SB 319 (2021-2022, single-subject, 100-67 House / 34-22 Senate) makes it so "no person shall carry a weapon unless he or she is a lawful weapons carrier", defined as "any person who is licensed or eligible for a license", which removes the permit to carry for eligible adults: "Repeal laws that require a permit or registration for a gun, such as the permit to carry". HB 1324 (2025-2026, single-subject, 87-76) repeals the silencer definition in O.C.G.A. 16-11-121 and takes silencers off the dangerous-weapons possession ban of 16-11-122, whose only exception (16-11-124) is registration "in accordance with the dictates of the National Firearms Act"; under R5-AZ that is removing an accessory from a registration law, rung 5. His other acts are loosening: Yeas on HB 218 and SB 259 (rung 4) and HB 1018 (direction-only); his campaign pledge to "secure Second Amendment rights" is rhetorical. Yea on HB 1023 (2025-2026, weapon detection in school buildings, 151-11) is near-unanimous and gated; it is also a school-safety measure (adjacent) and "weapon" there is wider than firearms. Yea on SB 204 (2025-2026) is on a multi-subject bill whose gun part is state preemption (H12, adjacent), so it shows no rung. No rung 1-3 act, so the furthest act is rung 5.$q$,
   $q$Yea on the House passage of SB 319 (2022-03-30, counted once) and Yea on HB 1324 (2026-03-06). SB 319 (2021-2022, single-subject, 100-67 House / 34-22 Senate) makes it so "no person shall carry a weapon unless he or she is a lawful weapons carrier", defined as "any person who is licensed or eligible for a license", which removes the permit to carry for eligible adults: "Repeal laws that require a permit or registration for a gun, such as the permit to carry". HB 1324 (2025-2026, single-subject, 87-76) repeals the silencer definition in O.C.G.A. 16-11-121 and takes silencers off the dangerous-weapons possession ban of 16-11-122, whose only exception (16-11-124) is registration "in accordance with the dictates of the National Firearms Act"; that is removing an accessory from a registration law, rung 5. His other acts are loosening: Yeas on HB 218 and SB 259 (rung 4) and HB 1018 (direction-only); his campaign pledge to secure Second Amendment rights is rhetorical. Yea on HB 1023 (2025-2026, weapon detection in school buildings, 151-11) is near-unanimous and gated; it is also a school-safety measure (adjacent) and "weapon" there is wider than firearms. Yea on SB 204 (2025-2026) is on a multi-subject bill whose gun part is state preemption (adjacent), so it shows no rung. No rung 1-3 act, so the furthest act is rung 5.$q$),
  ('0b95813c-bb9b-426d-91e1-e5102ef21543', 5,
   $q$Yea on SB 319 (2021-22), Senate final vote 2022-04-01 agreeing to the House substitute, 34-22 (filed under both GA-SB-319-2021 and GA-SB-319-2022; counted once): the enacted text says "no person shall carry a weapon unless he or she is a lawful weapons carrier" and defines that as "any person who is licensed or eligible for a license", so the licence to carry a handgun is no longer required; he is also a listed sponsor of SB 319; and Yea on SB 499 (2025-26), Senate passage 2026-03-06, 30-20: it repeals the silencer definition in O.C.G.A. 16-11-121 and strikes "silencer" from the 16-11-122 possession ban, whose exception is NFA registration; under R5-AZ that is removing an accessory from a registration law. Both meet rung 5, "Repeal laws that require a permit or registration for a gun, such as the permit to carry". His other acts (HB 218, HB 280, HB 292, SB 259, SB 478 sponsorship) are rung 4 loosenings and he has no rung 1-3 act; his floor line about Ukrainians' right to bear arms names no rule.$q$,
   $q$Yea on SB 319 (2021-22), Senate final vote 2022-04-01 agreeing to the House substitute, 34-22 (filed under both GA-SB-319-2021 and GA-SB-319-2022; counted once): the enacted text says "no person shall carry a weapon unless he or she is a lawful weapons carrier" and defines that as "any person who is licensed or eligible for a license", so the licence to carry a handgun is no longer required; he is also a listed sponsor of SB 319; and Yea on SB 499 (2025-26), Senate passage 2026-03-06, 30-20: it repeals the silencer definition in O.C.G.A. 16-11-121 and strikes "silencer" from the 16-11-122 possession ban, whose exception is NFA registration; that is removing an accessory from a registration law. Both meet rung 5, "Repeal laws that require a permit or registration for a gun, such as the permit to carry". His other acts (HB 218, HB 280, HB 292, SB 259, SB 478 sponsorship) are rung 4 loosenings and he has no rung 1-3 act; his floor line about Ukrainians' right to bear arms names no rule.$q$),
  ('0c210cd6-bc2f-4bf4-8a53-b9dcbfee6452', 5,
   $q$Rung 5, "Repeal laws that require a permit or registration for a gun, such as the permit to carry": a Yea on SB 319 (2021-22; House Vote #817, 2022-03-30, 100-67; single-subject, not gated), whose operative text defines a "lawful weapons carrier" as "any person who is licensed or eligible for a license" and provides "no person shall carry a weapon unless he or she is a lawful weapons carrier", so a carry licence is no longer required (the store holds this vote twice, as GA-SB-319-2021 and GA-SB-319-2022; it is counted once), and a Yea on HB 1324 (2025-26; House Vote #665, 2026-03-06, 87-76; single-subject, not gated), which strikes "silencer" from O.C.G.A. 16-11-122 ("No person shall have in his or her possession any sawed-off shotgun, sawed-off rifle, machine gun, or dangerous weapon" … "except as provided in Code Section 16-11-124"), a ban whose exception is items registered "in accordance with the dictates of the National Firearms Act": removing an accessory from a registration law (R5-AZ). Its other carry votes on the same side (rung 4 acts that keep the permit, e.g. HB 218 reciprocity, SB 259 removing places of worship from the barred list) sit under the furthest act; HB 1018 (2023-24) is a merchant-code rule (adjacent) plus a bar on a gun-owner registry where none exists (direction-only, R5-L); HB 1023 (weapon detection at school entrances, "weapon" wider than firearms) is a school-safety measure (adjacent) and is gated near-unanimous (151-11); SB 204 (2025) is multi-subject and its gun part is preemption (adjacent). No rung 1-3 act is on file.$q$,
   $q$Rung 5, "Repeal laws that require a permit or registration for a gun, such as the permit to carry": a Yea on SB 319 (2021-22; House Vote #817, 2022-03-30, 100-67; single-subject, not gated), whose operative text defines a "lawful weapons carrier" as "any person who is licensed or eligible for a license" and provides "no person shall carry a weapon unless he or she is a lawful weapons carrier", so a carry licence is no longer required (the store holds this vote twice, as GA-SB-319-2021 and GA-SB-319-2022; it is counted once), and a Yea on HB 1324 (2025-26; House Vote #665, 2026-03-06, 87-76; single-subject, not gated), which strikes "silencer" from O.C.G.A. 16-11-122 ("No person shall have in his or her possession any sawed-off shotgun, sawed-off rifle, machine gun, or dangerous weapon" … "except as provided in Code Section 16-11-124"), a ban whose exception is items registered "in accordance with the dictates of the National Firearms Act": removing an accessory from a registration law. Its other carry votes on the same side (rung 4 acts that keep the permit, e.g. HB 218 reciprocity, SB 259 removing places of worship from the barred list) sit under the furthest act; HB 1018 (2023-24) is a merchant-code rule (adjacent) plus a bar on a gun-owner registry where none exists (direction-only); HB 1023 (weapon detection at school entrances, "weapon" wider than firearms) is a school-safety measure (adjacent) and is gated near-unanimous (151-11); SB 204 (2025) is multi-subject and its gun part is preemption (adjacent). No rung 1-3 act is on file.$q$),
  ('0df6f5b5-44f5-4e3a-8359-d032a379cf0e', 5,
   $q$Yea on GA SB 319 (2021-2022, Act 596) provides that "no person shall carry a weapon unless he or she is a lawful weapons carrier", where a lawful weapons carrier is any person "licensed or eligible for a license", which ends the licence requirement to carry for eligible adults (House, 2022-03-30, 100-67, not gated), and Yea on GA HB 1324 (2025-2026) takes silencers off Georgia's dangerous-weapons ban (O.C.G.A. 16-11-122, "No person shall have in his or her possession any sawed-off shotgun, sawed-off rifle, machine gun, or dangerous weapon, or silencer except as provided in Code Section 16-11-124", with "or silencer" struck and the silencer definition in 16-11-121(7) repealed), a ban whose exception is possession "registered" … "in accordance with the dictates of the National Firearms Act"; under R5-AZ that is removing an accessory from a registration law (2026-03-06, 87-76). Both meet rung 5, "Repeal laws that require a permit or registration for a gun, such as the permit to carry". His other acts (Yeas and sponsorships on HB 218, HB 280, HB 859, HB 292, HB 1060, SB 259, HB 1378, HB 560: reciprocity, campus and worship carry) are rung 4 and sit below the furthest act; no rung 1-3 act is on file.$q$,
   $q$Yea on GA SB 319 (2021-2022, Act 596) provides that "no person shall carry a weapon unless he or she is a lawful weapons carrier", where a lawful weapons carrier is any person "licensed or eligible for a license", which ends the licence requirement to carry for eligible adults (House, 2022-03-30, 100-67, not gated), and Yea on GA HB 1324 (2025-2026) takes silencers off Georgia's dangerous-weapons ban (O.C.G.A. 16-11-122, "No person shall have in his or her possession any sawed-off shotgun, sawed-off rifle, machine gun, or dangerous weapon, or silencer except as provided in Code Section 16-11-124", with "or silencer" struck and the silencer definition in 16-11-121(7) repealed), a ban whose exception is possession "registered" … "in accordance with the dictates of the National Firearms Act"; that is removing an accessory from a registration law (2026-03-06, 87-76). Both meet rung 5, "Repeal laws that require a permit or registration for a gun, such as the permit to carry". His other acts (Yeas and sponsorships on HB 218, HB 280, HB 859, HB 292, HB 1060, SB 259, HB 1378, HB 560: reciprocity, campus and worship carry) are rung 4 and sit below the furthest act; no rung 1-3 act is on file.$q$),
  ('0f2833fc-f8b1-4d11-9721-de31972f3bb8', 5,
   $q$Rung 5, "Repeal laws that require a permit or registration for a gun, such as the permit to carry": a Yea on SB 319 (2021-22; House Vote #817, 2022-03-30, 100-67; single-subject, not gated), whose operative text defines a "lawful weapons carrier" as "any person who is licensed or eligible for a license" and provides "no person shall carry a weapon unless he or she is a lawful weapons carrier", so a carry licence is no longer required (the store holds this vote twice, as GA-SB-319-2021 and GA-SB-319-2022; it is counted once), and a Yea on HB 1324 (2025-26; House Vote #665, 2026-03-06, 87-76; single-subject, not gated), which strikes "silencer" from O.C.G.A. 16-11-122 ("No person shall have in his or her possession any sawed-off shotgun, sawed-off rifle, machine gun, or dangerous weapon" … "except as provided in Code Section 16-11-124"), a ban whose exception is items registered "in accordance with the dictates of the National Firearms Act": removing an accessory from a registration law (R5-AZ). Her Yea on SB 259 (rung 4) is on the same side; her 2022 No on HB 218 is a No on a different bill and cancels nothing; her sponsorship of HB 121 (bar on enforcing federal gun rules) is adjacent (closure C2). HB 1018 (2023-24) is a merchant-code rule (adjacent) plus a bar on a gun-owner registry where none exists (direction-only, R5-L); HB 1023 (weapon detection at school entrances, "weapon" wider than firearms) is a school-safety measure (adjacent) and is gated near-unanimous (151-11); SB 204 (2025) is multi-subject and its gun part is preemption (adjacent). No rung 1-3 act is on file. Her 2025 column ("reducing restrictions on where and how responsible citizens can carry") shows the loosening side only.$q$,
   $q$Rung 5, "Repeal laws that require a permit or registration for a gun, such as the permit to carry": a Yea on SB 319 (2021-22; House Vote #817, 2022-03-30, 100-67; single-subject, not gated), whose operative text defines a "lawful weapons carrier" as "any person who is licensed or eligible for a license" and provides "no person shall carry a weapon unless he or she is a lawful weapons carrier", so a carry licence is no longer required (the store holds this vote twice, as GA-SB-319-2021 and GA-SB-319-2022; it is counted once), and a Yea on HB 1324 (2025-26; House Vote #665, 2026-03-06, 87-76; single-subject, not gated), which strikes "silencer" from O.C.G.A. 16-11-122 ("No person shall have in his or her possession any sawed-off shotgun, sawed-off rifle, machine gun, or dangerous weapon" … "except as provided in Code Section 16-11-124"), a ban whose exception is items registered "in accordance with the dictates of the National Firearms Act": removing an accessory from a registration law. Her Yea on SB 259 (rung 4) is on the same side; her 2022 No on HB 218 is a No on a different bill and cancels nothing; her sponsorship of HB 121 (bar on enforcing federal gun rules) is adjacent. HB 1018 (2023-24) is a merchant-code rule (adjacent) plus a bar on a gun-owner registry where none exists (direction-only); HB 1023 (weapon detection at school entrances, "weapon" wider than firearms) is a school-safety measure (adjacent) and is gated near-unanimous (151-11); SB 204 (2025) is multi-subject and its gun part is preemption (adjacent). No rung 1-3 act is on file. Her 2025 column, which favours fewer limits on where and how people may carry, shows the loosening side only.$q$),
  ('10ed2260-bf8c-41f4-868b-78ef107abc94', 5,
   $q$Furthest act is rung 5. He voted Yea on HB 1324. GA-HB-1324-2025 (single-subject) strikes "or silencer" from O.C.G.A. 16-11-122 ("No person shall have in his or her possession any sawed-off shotgun, sawed-off rifle, machine gun, or dangerous weapon, or silencer") and 16-11-123, a ban whose only exception (16-11-124) is registration "in accordance with the dictates of the National Firearms Act". Under R5-AZ, taking a type of firearm item off a ban whose only exception is federal registration is a rung 5 act. House passage 2026-03-06, 87-76, census gate none. Identity: the searcher found that the bill texts of HB 1018 and HB 1324 are by 'Ridley of the 6th' (Jason Ridley), so the 'prime sponsor' line on file for HB 1018 is Jason Ridley's and is not used; Jordan Ridley ('of the 22nd') is a co-sponsor of HB 1018 only, which under R5-L is direction-only on the loosening side. His HB 1324 floor vote is his own roll-call vote and is not affected by the sponsor mix-up. His voter-guide answers ('None, they violate the 2nd Amendment') and campaign-site words name no rung act and show direction only. His HB 1023 No is adjacent and gated; his SB 204 Yea is multi-subject. Nothing conflicts with rung 5. Evidence basis: record.$q$,
