  ('267a7590-9ce3-4151-9d40-d0db381d664c', 5,
   $q$Yea on the House passage of SB 319 (2022-03-30, counted once) and, now a senator, Yea on SB 499 (2026-03-06); a record from either chamber of the same legislature counts (V5). SB 319 (2021-2022, single-subject, 100-67 House / 34-22 Senate) makes it so "no person shall carry a weapon unless he or she is a lawful weapons carrier", defined as "any person who is licensed or eligible for a license", which removes the permit to carry for eligible adults: "Repeal laws that require a permit or registration for a gun, such as the permit to carry". SB 499 (2025-2026, single-subject, Senate 30-20) repeals "paragraph (7) of Code Section 16-11-121" and takes silencers off the 16-11-122 dangerous-weapons ban whose exception is NFA registration; under R5-AZ that is removing an accessory from a registration law, rung 5 (its new crime for prohibited persons holding a silencer is a gun crime and does not make it multi-subject). His other acts are loosening: Yeas on HB 292 and HB 280 (2017), HB 218 and SB 259 (2022) (rung 4) and HB 1018 (direction-only). Yea on SB 204 (2025-2026) is on a multi-subject bill whose gun part is state preemption (H12, adjacent), so it shows no rung. No rung 1-3 act, so the furthest act is rung 5.$q$,
