   $q$Sponsor of SB 319 and Yea on its final Senate vote (2022-04-01, agree to the House substitute; the store holds it under two instrument files, counted once); sponsor of SB 499 and Yea on its passage (2026-03-06). SB 319 (2021-2022, single-subject, 100-67 House / 34-22 Senate) makes it so "no person shall carry a weapon unless he or she is a lawful weapons carrier", defined as "any person who is licensed or eligible for a license", which removes the permit to carry for eligible adults: "Repeal laws that require a permit or registration for a gun, such as the permit to carry". SB 499 (2025-2026, single-subject, Senate 30-20) repeals "paragraph (7) of Code Section 16-11-121" and takes silencers off the 16-11-122 dangerous-weapons ban whose exception is NFA registration; under R5-AZ that is removing an accessory from a registration law, rung 5 (its new crime for prohibited persons holding a silencer is a gun crime and does not make it multi-subject). His other acts are on the loosening side: Yeas on HB 218 (reciprocity) and HB 1018, sponsorships of SB 214 (no carry bans in emergencies) and SB 478 (park carry; read as a carry widening, not relied on). He abstained on SB 259 (2022-02-28), which shows nothing. His floor words on a sales-tax holiday bill are rhetorical. Yea on SB 204 (2025-2026) is on a multi-subject bill whose gun part is state preemption (H12, adjacent), so it shows no rung. No rung 1-3 act, so the furthest act is rung 5.$q$,
