  ('34010d05-e36a-4e4a-8f5c-35bc0cf0e561', 5,
   $q$Rung 5, "Repeal laws that require a permit or registration for a gun, such as the permit to carry": a Yea on SB 319 (2021-22; House Vote #817, 2022-03-30, 100-67; single-subject, not gated), whose operative text defines a "lawful weapons carrier" as "any person who is licensed or eligible for a license" and provides "no person shall carry a weapon unless he or she is a lawful weapons carrier", so a carry licence is no longer required (the store holds this vote twice, as GA-SB-319-2021 and GA-SB-319-2022; it is counted once), and a Yea on HB 1324 (2025-26; House Vote #665, 2026-03-06, 87-76; single-subject, not gated), which strikes "silencer" from O.C.G.A. 16-11-122 ("No person shall have in his or her possession any sawed-off shotgun, sawed-off rifle, machine gun, or dangerous weapon" … "except as provided in Code Section 16-11-124"), a ban whose exception is items registered "in accordance with the dictates of the National Firearms Act": removing an accessory from a registration law (R5-AZ). Yeas on rung 4 carry bills (HB 218, SB 259, HB 292, HB 859, HB 1060) are on the same side and sit under the furthest act; his 2017 No on HB 280 (campus carry) is a No on a different bill and cancels nothing. HB 1018 (2023-24) is a merchant-code rule (adjacent) plus a bar on a gun-owner registry where none exists (direction-only, R5-L); HB 1023 (weapon detection at school entrances, "weapon" wider than firearms) is a school-safety measure (adjacent) and is gated near-unanimous (151-11); SB 204 (2025) is multi-subject and its gun part is preemption (adjacent). No rung 1-3 act is on file. His 2024 newsletter describes a tax credit for safety courses and secure-storage devices: taxes and storage are adjacent, and it states no rung.$q$,
   $q$Rung 5, "Repeal laws that require a permit or registration for a gun, such as the permit to carry": a Yea on SB 319 (2021-22; House Vote #817, 2022-03-30, 100-67; single-subject, not gated), whose operative text defines a "lawful weapons carrier" as "any person who is licensed or eligible for a license" and provides "no person shall carry a weapon unless he or she is a lawful weapons carrier", so a carry licence is no longer required (the store holds this vote twice, as GA-SB-319-2021 and GA-SB-319-2022; it is counted once), and a Yea on HB 1324 (2025-26; House Vote #665, 2026-03-06, 87-76; single-subject, not gated), which strikes "silencer" from O.C.G.A. 16-11-122 ("No person shall have in his or her possession any sawed-off shotgun, sawed-off rifle, machine gun, or dangerous weapon" … "except as provided in Code Section 16-11-124"), a ban whose exception is items registered "in accordance with the dictates of the National Firearms Act": removing an accessory from a registration law. Yeas on rung 4 carry bills (HB 218, SB 259, HB 292, HB 859, HB 1060) are on the same side and sit under the furthest act; his 2017 No on HB 280 (campus carry) is a No on a different bill and cancels nothing. HB 1018 (2023-24) is a merchant-code rule (adjacent) plus a bar on a gun-owner registry where none exists (direction-only); HB 1023 (weapon detection at school entrances, "weapon" wider than firearms) is a school-safety measure (adjacent) and is gated near-unanimous (151-11); SB 204 (2025) is multi-subject and its gun part is preemption (adjacent). No rung 1-3 act is on file. His 2024 newsletter describes a tax credit for safety courses and secure-storage devices: taxes and storage are adjacent, and it states no rung.$q$),
  ('3818cfdb-7899-475d-a941-1176961c2534', 5,
   $q$Yea on HB 1324 House passage, 2026-03-06. HB 1324 (2025-2026) is single-subject and its House passage was 87-76 (not gated). It repeals the silencer definition in O.C.G.A. 16-11-121 and strikes "or silencer" from 16-11-122 ("No person shall have in his or her possession any sawed-off shotgun, sawed-off rifle, machine gun, or dangerous weapon, or silencer except as provided in Code Section 16-11-124"), whose exception is items registered "in accordance with the dictates of the National Firearms Act". Under R5-AZ that takes an accessory off a registration law: rung 5, "Repeal laws that require a permit or registration for a gun". Her newsletter words on HB 1023 (weapon detection at school entrances) are about school safety, adjacent under the annex scope line, and the HB 1023 vote is gated (151-11); SB 204 is adjacent preemption. No rung 1-3 act.$q$,
   $q$Yea on HB 1324 House passage, 2026-03-06. HB 1324 (2025-2026) is single-subject and its House passage was 87-76 (not gated). It repeals the silencer definition in O.C.G.A. 16-11-121 and strikes "or silencer" from 16-11-122 ("No person shall have in his or her possession any sawed-off shotgun, sawed-off rifle, machine gun, or dangerous weapon, or silencer except as provided in Code Section 16-11-124"), whose exception is items registered "in accordance with the dictates of the National Firearms Act". That takes an accessory off a registration law: rung 5, "Repeal laws that require a permit or registration for a gun". Her newsletter words on HB 1023 (weapon detection at school entrances) are about school safety, adjacent under the annex scope line, and the HB 1023 vote is gated (151-11); SB 204 is adjacent preemption. No rung 1-3 act.$q$),
  ('3a1d3974-0728-49ae-926f-587a40f45437', 5,
   $q$Rung 5 (repeal laws that require a permit or registration for a gun, such as the permit to carry). He voted Yea on GA SB 319 in the House (2022-03-30, 100-67), whose enacted text says 'no person shall carry a weapon unless he or she is a lawful weapons carrier' (anyone 'licensed or eligible for a license'), ending the carry-permit requirement, and Yea on HB 1324 (2026-03-06, 87-76), which strikes 'silencer' from the O.C.G.A. 16-11-122 ban whose only exception is NFA registration (R5-AZ). His other votes (HB 218, HB 280, HB 292, SB 259, HB 1018) are on the loosening side; no rung 1-3 act. His own words on a safe-storage tax credit (not in the store as a bill) and on 'the right to defend yourself' name no rung act.$q$,
