  ('4350ece0-e942-4e38-93fd-443ae00a374c', 5,
   $q$Yea on SB 319 (2021-22), House passage 2022-03-30, 100-67 (one roll call, filed under both GA-SB-319-2021 and GA-SB-319-2022; counted once): the enacted text says "no person shall carry a weapon unless he or she is a lawful weapons carrier" and defines that as "any person who is licensed or eligible for a license", so the licence to carry a handgun is no longer required; and Yea on HB 1324 (2025-26), House passage 2026-03-06, 87-76: it repeals the silencer definition in O.C.G.A. 16-11-121 and strikes "silencer" from the 16-11-122 possession ban, whose exception is items registered "in accordance with the dictates of the National Firearms Act"; under R5-AZ that is removing an accessory from a registration law. Both meet rung 5, "Repeal laws that require a permit or registration for a gun, such as the permit to carry". His other acts (HB 218, SB 259) are rung 4 and HB 1018 direction-only; he has no rung 1-3 act. His voter-guide answers ("law abiding citizens should have no restrictions") agree but are not needed.$q$,
   $q$Yea on SB 319 (2021-22), House passage 2022-03-30, 100-67 (one roll call, filed under both GA-SB-319-2021 and GA-SB-319-2022; counted once): the enacted text says "no person shall carry a weapon unless he or she is a lawful weapons carrier" and defines that as "any person who is licensed or eligible for a license", so the licence to carry a handgun is no longer required; and Yea on HB 1324 (2025-26), House passage 2026-03-06, 87-76: it repeals the silencer definition in O.C.G.A. 16-11-121 and strikes "silencer" from the 16-11-122 possession ban, whose exception is items registered "in accordance with the dictates of the National Firearms Act"; that is removing an accessory from a registration law. Both meet rung 5, "Repeal laws that require a permit or registration for a gun, such as the permit to carry". His other acts (HB 218, SB 259) are rung 4 and HB 1018 direction-only; he has no rung 1-3 act. His voter-guide answers, which oppose any limits for law-abiding gun owners, agree but are not needed.$q$),
  ('4420828d-3c43-45fb-ac4a-95eb1e3ad916', 5,
   $q$Furthest act is rung 5. He voted Yea on HB 1324. GA-HB-1324-2025 (single-subject) strikes "or silencer" from O.C.G.A. 16-11-122 ("No person shall have in his or her possession any sawed-off shotgun, sawed-off rifle, machine gun, or dangerous weapon, or silencer") and 16-11-123, a ban whose only exception (16-11-124) is registration "in accordance with the dictates of the National Firearms Act". Under R5-AZ, taking a type of firearm item off a ban whose only exception is federal registration is a rung 5 act. House passage 2026-03-06, 87-76, census gate none. His HB 1023 Yea is adjacent and gated near-unanimous; his SB 204 Yea is multi-subject (direction only). His campaign-site line on removing 'illegal guns' is third-person text and names no rung act. Nothing conflicts with rung 5. Evidence basis: record.$q$,
   $q$Furthest act is rung 5. He voted Yea on HB 1324. GA-HB-1324-2025 (single-subject) strikes "or silencer" from O.C.G.A. 16-11-122 ("No person shall have in his or her possession any sawed-off shotgun, sawed-off rifle, machine gun, or dangerous weapon, or silencer") and 16-11-123, a ban whose only exception (16-11-124) is registration "in accordance with the dictates of the National Firearms Act". Taking a type of firearm item off a ban whose only exception is federal registration is a rung 5 act. House passage 2026-03-06, 87-76, census gate none. His HB 1023 Yea is adjacent and gated near-unanimous; his SB 204 Yea is multi-subject (direction only). His campaign-site line on removing 'illegal guns' is third-person text and names no rung act. Nothing conflicts with rung 5. Evidence basis: record.$q$),
  ('4a2dbdf1-1076-4b50-9e18-b296a0578f53', 5,
   $q$Furthest act is rung 5. She voted Yea on SB 319 and on HB 1324. GA-SB-319-2021 (single-subject; Georgia Constitutional Carry Act) lets a lawful weapons carrier "have or carry on his or her person a weapon or long gun" … "without a valid weapons carry license": it repeals the requirement of a permit to carry, which is rung 5 ("Repeal laws that require a permit" … "such as the permit to carry"). House passage 2022-03-30, 100-67, census gate none. GA-HB-1324-2025 (single-subject) strikes "or silencer" from O.C.G.A. 16-11-122 ("No person shall have in his or her possession any sawed-off shotgun, sawed-off rifle, machine gun, or dangerous weapon, or silencer") and 16-11-123, a ban whose only exception (16-11-124) is registration "in accordance with the dictates of the National Firearms Act". Under R5-AZ, taking a type of firearm item off a ban whose only exception is federal registration is a rung 5 act. House passage 2026-03-06, 87-76, census gate none. The GA-SB-319-2022 finding (7017959d...) duplicates the same House roll call and has no instrument file, so it is counted once, through GA-SB-319-2021. The person's other gun Yeas (HB 218 reciprocity, HB 280 campus carry for licence holders, SB 259, HB 292) are rung 4 or loosening direction-only acts; none is a rung 1-3 act, so nothing conflicts with rung 5. Her HB 560 sponsorship (places of worship off the banned list, carry-licence database ban, no emergency seizures) is loosening, not a rung 1-3 act. Her storage words (a tax-credit bill) are adjacent and her 2nd Amendment campaign words name no rung. GA-HB-1023-2025 (weapon detection in school buildings; "weapon" there is wider than firearms) is read as a school-safety measure, adjacent, and its vote was near-unanimous (151-11), so it is gated either way. GA-SB-204-2025 is multi-subject (criminal charging by accusation plus state preemption), so its vote shows a direction at most (V4.1). GA-HB-1018-2023: under R5-L the merchant-code part is adjacent and the registry bar, where no registry exists, is direction-only (loosening side); not a rung act. Evidence basis: record.$q$,
   $q$Furthest act is rung 5. She voted Yea on SB 319 and on HB 1324. GA-SB-319-2021 (single-subject; Georgia Constitutional Carry Act) lets a lawful weapons carrier "have or carry on his or her person a weapon or long gun" … "without a valid weapons carry license": it repeals the requirement of a permit to carry, which is rung 5 ("Repeal laws that require a permit" … "such as the permit to carry"). House passage 2022-03-30, 100-67, census gate none. GA-HB-1324-2025 (single-subject) strikes "or silencer" from O.C.G.A. 16-11-122 ("No person shall have in his or her possession any sawed-off shotgun, sawed-off rifle, machine gun, or dangerous weapon, or silencer") and 16-11-123, a ban whose only exception (16-11-124) is registration "in accordance with the dictates of the National Firearms Act". Taking a type of firearm item off a ban whose only exception is federal registration is a rung 5 act. House passage 2026-03-06, 87-76, census gate none. The GA-SB-319-2022 finding (7017959d...) duplicates the same House roll call and has no instrument file, so it is counted once, through GA-SB-319-2021. The person's other gun Yeas (HB 218 reciprocity, HB 280 campus carry for licence holders, SB 259, HB 292) are rung 4 or loosening direction-only acts; none is a rung 1-3 act, so nothing conflicts with rung 5. Her HB 560 sponsorship (places of worship off the banned list, carry-licence database ban, no emergency seizures) is loosening, not a rung 1-3 act. Her storage words (a tax-credit bill) are adjacent and her 2nd Amendment campaign words name no rung. GA-HB-1023-2025 (weapon detection in school buildings; "weapon" there is wider than firearms) is read as a school-safety measure, adjacent, and its vote was near-unanimous (151-11), so it is gated either way. GA-SB-204-2025 is multi-subject (criminal charging by accusation plus state preemption), so its vote shows a direction at most (V4.1). GA-HB-1018-2023: the merchant-code part is adjacent and the registry bar, where no registry exists, is direction-only (loosening side); not a rung act. Evidence basis: record.$q$),
