  ('530eaa75-22b5-45e7-ba1c-c39cb8237786', 5,
   $q$Furthest act is rung 5. He voted Yea on SB 319 and on HB 1324. GA-SB-319-2021 (single-subject; Georgia Constitutional Carry Act) lets a lawful weapons carrier "have or carry on his or her person a weapon or long gun" … "without a valid weapons carry license": it repeals the requirement of a permit to carry, which is rung 5 ("Repeal laws that require a permit" … "such as the permit to carry"). House passage 2022-03-30, 100-67, census gate none. GA-HB-1324-2025 (single-subject) strikes "or silencer" from O.C.G.A. 16-11-122 ("No person shall have in his or her possession any sawed-off shotgun, sawed-off rifle, machine gun, or dangerous weapon, or silencer") and 16-11-123, a ban whose only exception (16-11-124) is registration "in accordance with the dictates of the National Firearms Act". Under R5-AZ, taking a type of firearm item off a ban whose only exception is federal registration is a rung 5 act. House passage 2026-03-06, 87-76, census gate none. The GA-SB-319-2022 finding (7017959d...) duplicates the same House roll call and has no instrument file, so it is counted once, through GA-SB-319-2021. The person's other gun Yeas (HB 218 reciprocity, HB 280/HB 859 campus carry for licence holders, SB 259, HB 292, HB 1060) are rung 4 or loosening direction-only acts; none is a rung 1-3 act, so nothing conflicts with rung 5. GA-HB-1023-2025 (weapon detection in school buildings; "weapon" there is wider than firearms) is read as a school-safety measure, adjacent, and its vote was near-unanimous (151-11), so it is gated either way. GA-SB-204-2025 is multi-subject (criminal charging by accusation plus state preemption), so its vote shows a direction at most (V4.1). GA-HB-1018-2023: under R5-L the merchant-code part is adjacent and the registry bar, where no registry exists, is direction-only (loosening side); not a rung act. No own words on the topic were found. Evidence basis: record (counted state roll calls on bills in the store).$q$,
   $q$Furthest act is rung 5. He voted Yea on SB 319 and on HB 1324. GA-SB-319-2021 (single-subject; Georgia Constitutional Carry Act) lets a lawful weapons carrier "have or carry on his or her person a weapon or long gun" … "without a valid weapons carry license": it repeals the requirement of a permit to carry, which is rung 5 ("Repeal laws that require a permit" … "such as the permit to carry"). House passage 2022-03-30, 100-67, census gate none. GA-HB-1324-2025 (single-subject) strikes "or silencer" from O.C.G.A. 16-11-122 ("No person shall have in his or her possession any sawed-off shotgun, sawed-off rifle, machine gun, or dangerous weapon, or silencer") and 16-11-123, a ban whose only exception (16-11-124) is registration "in accordance with the dictates of the National Firearms Act". Taking a type of firearm item off a ban whose only exception is federal registration is a rung 5 act. House passage 2026-03-06, 87-76, census gate none. The GA-SB-319-2022 finding (7017959d...) duplicates the same House roll call and has no instrument file, so it is counted once, through GA-SB-319-2021. The person's other gun Yeas (HB 218 reciprocity, HB 280/HB 859 campus carry for licence holders, SB 259, HB 292, HB 1060) are rung 4 or loosening direction-only acts; none is a rung 1-3 act, so nothing conflicts with rung 5. GA-HB-1023-2025 (weapon detection in school buildings; "weapon" there is wider than firearms) is read as a school-safety measure, adjacent, and its vote was near-unanimous (151-11), so it is gated either way. GA-SB-204-2025 is multi-subject (criminal charging by accusation plus state preemption), so its vote shows a direction at most (V4.1). GA-HB-1018-2023: the merchant-code part is adjacent and the registry bar, where no registry exists, is direction-only (loosening side); not a rung act. No own words on the topic were found. Evidence basis: record (counted state roll calls on bills in the store).$q$),
  ('5c7b1cf2-7e21-425a-85ea-b7823d71cfcf', 5,
   $q$Yea on SB 319 House passage, 2022-03-30. SB 319 (2021-2022, the Constitutional Carry Act; stored twice as GA-SB-319-2021 and GA-SB-319-2022, counted once) is single-subject and passed 100-67 in the House and 34-22 on the Senate agree vote (not gated). Its operative text makes the carrier a "lawful weapons carrier", meaning anyone "licensed or eligible for a license", and provides "no person shall carry a weapon unless he or she is a lawful weapons carrier", so the carry licence is no longer required: rung 5, "Repeal laws that require a permit or registration for a gun, such as the permit to carry". Yea on HB 1324 (2026) is a second rung 5 act (R5-AZ). Her Yeas on HB 218 and SB 259 are rung 4 acts on the same side; HB 1018 is direction-only (R5-L); her 2026 campaign line "Defend the Second Amendment so law-abiding citizens can protect their families and homes" shows a side only. No rung 1-3 act.$q$,
   $q$Yea on SB 319 House passage, 2022-03-30. SB 319 (2021-2022, the Constitutional Carry Act; stored twice as GA-SB-319-2021 and GA-SB-319-2022, counted once) is single-subject and passed 100-67 in the House and 34-22 on the Senate agree vote (not gated). Its operative text makes the carrier a "lawful weapons carrier", meaning anyone "licensed or eligible for a license", and provides "no person shall carry a weapon unless he or she is a lawful weapons carrier", so the carry licence is no longer required: rung 5, "Repeal laws that require a permit or registration for a gun, such as the permit to carry". Yea on HB 1324 (2026) is a second rung 5 act: it takes silencers off a ban whose only exception is federal registration. Her Yeas on HB 218 and SB 259 are rung 4 acts on the same side; HB 1018 is direction-only (its bar on a gun-owner registry where none exists repeals nothing); her 2026 campaign line about defending the Second Amendment shows a side only. No rung 1-3 act.$q$),
  ('5ed1bb77-2b50-4872-bdad-c76af7fb7913', 5,
   $q$He cast a Yea on SB 319 (2022 House Vote #817, 100-67; the same roll call is filed twice in the store and is counted once), whose operative text says 'no person shall carry a weapon unless he or she is a lawful weapons carrier' and defines a lawful weapons carrier as anyone 'licensed or eligible for a license', so the permit to carry is no longer required. That is rung 5: 'Repeal laws that require a permit or registration for a gun, such as the permit to carry'. He also cast a Yea on HB 1324 (2026 House Vote #665, 87-76), which strikes 'or silencer' from O.C.G.A. 16-11-122's dangerous-weapons ban, whose exception (16-11-124) is NFA registration, i.e. it takes an accessory off a registration law (annex R5-AZ). His Yeas on HB 218 and SB 259 are rung 4 acts on the same side. HB 1018 is direction-only and SB 204 is multi-subject. His campaign line ('support the 2nd Amendment') states no rung.$q$,
   $q$He cast a Yea on SB 319 (2022 House Vote #817, 100-67; the same roll call is filed twice in the store and is counted once), whose operative text says 'no person shall carry a weapon unless he or she is a lawful weapons carrier' and defines a lawful weapons carrier as anyone 'licensed or eligible for a license', so the permit to carry is no longer required. That is rung 5: 'Repeal laws that require a permit or registration for a gun, such as the permit to carry'. He also cast a Yea on HB 1324 (2026 House Vote #665, 87-76), which strikes 'or silencer' from O.C.G.A. 16-11-122's dangerous-weapons ban, whose exception (16-11-124) is NFA registration, i.e. it takes an accessory off a registration law. His Yeas on HB 218 and SB 259 are rung 4 acts on the same side. HB 1018 is direction-only and SB 204 is multi-subject. His campaign line ('support the 2nd Amendment') states no rung.$q$),
  ('654e8fe4-70a9-45c0-8ffa-62480aee1f16', 5,
   $q$Yea on GA SB 319 (2021-2022, Act 596) provides that "no person shall carry a weapon unless he or she is a lawful weapons carrier", where a lawful weapons carrier is any person "licensed or eligible for a license", which ends the licence requirement to carry for eligible adults (Senate final vote agreeing to the House version, 2022-04-01, 34-22; the same vote appears under two instrument ids and is counted once), and Yea on GA SB 499 (2025-2026) repeals the silencer definition in O.C.G.A. 16-11-121(7) and strikes "or silencer" from 16-11-122's dangerous-weapons ban, whose exception (16-11-124) is NFA registration; under R5-AZ that is removing an accessory from a registration law (Senate, 2026-03-06, 30-20). Both meet rung 5, "Repeal laws that require a permit or registration for a gun". His other Yeas (HB 218, SB 259, HB 280, HB 859, HB 1060) are rung 4, below the furthest act; HB 292 was an abstention. His 2024 quote on gun safes ("we need guns safe") is about storage (adjacent). No rung 1-3 act is on file.$q$,
   $q$Yea on GA SB 319 (2021-2022, Act 596) provides that "no person shall carry a weapon unless he or she is a lawful weapons carrier", where a lawful weapons carrier is any person "licensed or eligible for a license", which ends the licence requirement to carry for eligible adults (Senate final vote agreeing to the House version, 2022-04-01, 34-22; the same vote appears under two instrument ids and is counted once), and Yea on GA SB 499 (2025-2026) repeals the silencer definition in O.C.G.A. 16-11-121(7) and strikes "or silencer" from 16-11-122's dangerous-weapons ban, whose exception (16-11-124) is NFA registration; that is removing an accessory from a registration law (Senate, 2026-03-06, 30-20). Both meet rung 5, "Repeal laws that require a permit or registration for a gun". His other Yeas (HB 218, SB 259, HB 280, HB 859, HB 1060) are rung 4, below the furthest act; HB 292 was an abstention. His 2024 words on gun safes are about storage (adjacent). No rung 1-3 act is on file.$q$),
  ('695f9da5-9744-4025-b6bd-aeb861242efa', 5,
   $q$He is a listed sponsor of SB 499 and voted Yea on its Senate passage, 2026-03-06. SB 499 (2025-2026) is single-subject and its Senate passage was 30-20 (not gated). It repeals the silencer definition in O.C.G.A. 16-11-121 and strikes silencers from the 16-11-122 dangerous-weapons ban, whose exception (16-11-124) is NFA registration, keeping a silencer offence only for people "prohibited from possessing or receiving a firearm". Under R5-AZ that takes an accessory off a registration law: rung 5, "Repeal laws that require a permit or registration for a gun". SB 204 is multi-subject preemption (adjacent). No rung 1-3 act.$q$,
   $q$He is a listed sponsor of SB 499 and voted Yea on its Senate passage, 2026-03-06. SB 499 (2025-2026) is single-subject and its Senate passage was 30-20 (not gated). It repeals the silencer definition in O.C.G.A. 16-11-121 and strikes silencers from the 16-11-122 dangerous-weapons ban, whose exception (16-11-124) is NFA registration, keeping a silencer offence only for people "prohibited from possessing or receiving a firearm". That takes an accessory off a registration law: rung 5, "Repeal laws that require a permit or registration for a gun". SB 204 is multi-subject preemption (adjacent). No rung 1-3 act.$q$),
