   $q$He cast a Yea on SB 319 (2022 House Vote #817, 100-67; the same roll call is filed twice in the store and is counted once), whose operative text says 'no person shall carry a weapon unless he or she is a lawful weapons carrier' and defines a lawful weapons carrier as anyone 'licensed or eligible for a license', so the permit to carry is no longer required. That is rung 5: 'Repeal laws that require a permit or registration for a gun, such as the permit to carry'. He also cast a Yea on HB 1324 (2026 House Vote #665, 87-76), which strikes 'or silencer' from O.C.G.A. 16-11-122's dangerous-weapons ban, whose exception (16-11-124) is NFA registration, i.e. it takes an accessory off a registration law (annex R5-AZ). His Yeas on HB 218, HB 280 (campus carry) and SB 259 are rung 4 acts on the same side, so the furthest act governs. HB 1018 is direction-only and SB 204 is multi-subject.$q$,
   $q$He cast a Yea on SB 319 (2022 House Vote #817, 100-67; the same roll call is filed twice in the store and is counted once), whose operative text says 'no person shall carry a weapon unless he or she is a lawful weapons carrier' and defines a lawful weapons carrier as anyone 'licensed or eligible for a license', so the permit to carry is no longer required. That is rung 5: 'Repeal laws that require a permit or registration for a gun, such as the permit to carry'. He also cast a Yea on HB 1324 (2026 House Vote #665, 87-76), which strikes 'or silencer' from O.C.G.A. 16-11-122's dangerous-weapons ban, whose exception (16-11-124) is NFA registration, i.e. it takes an accessory off a registration law. His Yeas on HB 218, HB 280 (campus carry) and SB 259 are rung 4 acts on the same side, so the furthest act governs. HB 1018 is direction-only and SB 204 is multi-subject.$q$),
  ('6ffadf75-49b6-4223-8b32-cf17b3c0290b', 5,
   $q$Rung 5, "Repeal laws that require a permit or registration for a gun, such as the permit to carry": prime sponsor of SB 319 and a Yea on SB 319 (2021-22; Senate Vote #783, agree to House substitute, 2022-04-01, 34-22; single-subject, not gated), whose operative text defines a "lawful weapons carrier" as "any person who is licensed or eligible for a license" and provides "no person shall carry a weapon unless he or she is a lawful weapons carrier", so a carry licence is no longer required (the same final vote appears twice in the store and is counted once; the earlier third-reading vote is not the record); and SB 499 (2025-26; sponsor, and Yea on Senate Vote #686, 2026-03-06, 30-20; single-subject, not gated), which strikes "silencer" from the O.C.G.A. 16-11-122 possession ban whose exception is National Firearms Act registration, so that "The exemption under this paragraph does not include the possession of silencers" (R5-AZ: removing an accessory from a registration law). Other acts are on the same side or out of scope: Yeas on HB 218 and SB 259 and sponsorship of SB 214 (emergency carry) are rung 4 acts under the furthest act; sponsorship of SB 67 (bar on enforcing federal gun rules) is adjacent (closure C2); HB 1018, which he sponsored and voted for, is a merchant-code rule (adjacent) plus a registry bar where none exists (direction-only, R5-L); SB 204 is multi-subject preemption (adjacent). No rung 1-3 act is on file.$q$,
   $q$Rung 5, "Repeal laws that require a permit or registration for a gun, such as the permit to carry": prime sponsor of SB 319 and a Yea on SB 319 (2021-22; Senate Vote #783, agree to House substitute, 2022-04-01, 34-22; single-subject, not gated), whose operative text defines a "lawful weapons carrier" as "any person who is licensed or eligible for a license" and provides "no person shall carry a weapon unless he or she is a lawful weapons carrier", so a carry licence is no longer required (the same final vote appears twice in the store and is counted once; the earlier third-reading vote is not the record); and SB 499 (2025-26; sponsor, and Yea on Senate Vote #686, 2026-03-06, 30-20; single-subject, not gated), which strikes "silencer" from the O.C.G.A. 16-11-122 possession ban whose exception is National Firearms Act registration, so that "The exemption under this paragraph does not include the possession of silencers" (removing an accessory from a registration law). Other acts are on the same side or out of scope: Yeas on HB 218 and SB 259 and sponsorship of SB 214 (emergency carry) are rung 4 acts under the furthest act; sponsorship of SB 67 (bar on enforcing federal gun rules) is adjacent; HB 1018, which he sponsored and voted for, is a merchant-code rule (adjacent) plus a registry bar where none exists (direction-only); SB 204 is multi-subject preemption (adjacent). No rung 1-3 act is on file.$q$),
  ('707ce5ae-efe9-41f2-bc93-8792a9d8c020', 5,
   $q$Rung 5, "Repeal laws that require a permit or registration for a gun, such as the permit to carry": a Yea on SB 319 (2021-22; House Vote #817, 2022-03-30, 100-67; single-subject, not gated), whose operative text defines a "lawful weapons carrier" as "any person who is licensed or eligible for a license" and provides "no person shall carry a weapon unless he or she is a lawful weapons carrier", so a carry licence is no longer required (the store holds this vote twice, as GA-SB-319-2021 and GA-SB-319-2022; it is counted once), and a Yea on HB 1324 (2025-26; House Vote #665, 2026-03-06, 87-76; single-subject, not gated), which strikes "silencer" from O.C.G.A. 16-11-122 ("No person shall have in his or her possession any sawed-off shotgun, sawed-off rifle, machine gun, or dangerous weapon" … "except as provided in Code Section 16-11-124"), a ban whose exception is items registered "in accordance with the dictates of the National Firearms Act": removing an accessory from a registration law (R5-AZ). Its other carry votes on the same side (rung 4 acts that keep the permit, e.g. HB 218 reciprocity, SB 259 removing places of worship from the barred list) sit under the furthest act; HB 1018 (2023-24) is a merchant-code rule (adjacent) plus a bar on a gun-owner registry where none exists (direction-only, R5-L); HB 1023 (weapon detection at school entrances, "weapon" wider than firearms) is a school-safety measure (adjacent) and is gated near-unanimous (151-11); SB 204 (2025) is multi-subject and its gun part is preemption (adjacent). No rung 1-3 act is on file. His campaign-site passage is written in the third person ("He believes"), so it is not his own words (V1).$q$,
   $q$Rung 5, "Repeal laws that require a permit or registration for a gun, such as the permit to carry": a Yea on SB 319 (2021-22; House Vote #817, 2022-03-30, 100-67; single-subject, not gated), whose operative text defines a "lawful weapons carrier" as "any person who is licensed or eligible for a license" and provides "no person shall carry a weapon unless he or she is a lawful weapons carrier", so a carry licence is no longer required (the store holds this vote twice, as GA-SB-319-2021 and GA-SB-319-2022; it is counted once), and a Yea on HB 1324 (2025-26; House Vote #665, 2026-03-06, 87-76; single-subject, not gated), which strikes "silencer" from O.C.G.A. 16-11-122 ("No person shall have in his or her possession any sawed-off shotgun, sawed-off rifle, machine gun, or dangerous weapon" … "except as provided in Code Section 16-11-124"), a ban whose exception is items registered "in accordance with the dictates of the National Firearms Act": removing an accessory from a registration law. Its other carry votes on the same side (rung 4 acts that keep the permit, e.g. HB 218 reciprocity, SB 259 removing places of worship from the barred list) sit under the furthest act; HB 1018 (2023-24) is a merchant-code rule (adjacent) plus a bar on a gun-owner registry where none exists (direction-only); HB 1023 (weapon detection at school entrances, "weapon" wider than firearms) is a school-safety measure (adjacent) and is gated near-unanimous (151-11); SB 204 (2025) is multi-subject and its gun part is preemption (adjacent). No rung 1-3 act is on file. His campaign-site passage is written in the third person ("He believes"), so it is not his own words (V1).$q$),
