  ('7c216769-195a-4dff-90b1-e72e6f25f4c4', 5,
   $q$Yea on the House passage of SB 319 (2022-03-30; the store holds this one roll call under two instrument files, counted once) and Yea on, and sponsorship of, HB 1324 (2026-03-06). SB 319 (2021-2022, single-subject, 100-67 House / 34-22 Senate) makes it so "no person shall carry a weapon unless he or she is a lawful weapons carrier", defined as "any person who is licensed or eligible for a license", which removes the permit to carry for eligible adults: "Repeal laws that require a permit or registration for a gun, such as the permit to carry". HB 1324 (2025-2026, single-subject, 87-76) repeals the silencer definition in O.C.G.A. 16-11-121 and takes silencers off the dangerous-weapons possession ban of 16-11-122, whose only exception (16-11-124) is registration "in accordance with the dictates of the National Firearms Act"; under R5-AZ that is removing an accessory from a registration law, rung 5. His other acts are on the loosening side only: Yeas on HB 218 and SB 259 (carry widening, rung 4), HB 1018 and a sponsorship of HB 1454 (registry bans, direction-only under R5-L); Yea on HB 1023 (2025-2026, weapon detection in school buildings, 151-11) is near-unanimous and gated; it is also a school-safety measure (adjacent) and "weapon" there is wider than firearms. Yea on SB 204 (2025-2026) is on a multi-subject bill whose gun part is state preemption (H12, adjacent), so it shows no rung. No rung 1-3 act, so the furthest act is rung 5.$q$,
   $q$Yea on the House passage of SB 319 (2022-03-30; the store holds this one roll call under two instrument files, counted once) and Yea on, and sponsorship of, HB 1324 (2026-03-06). SB 319 (2021-2022, single-subject, 100-67 House / 34-22 Senate) makes it so "no person shall carry a weapon unless he or she is a lawful weapons carrier", defined as "any person who is licensed or eligible for a license", which removes the permit to carry for eligible adults: "Repeal laws that require a permit or registration for a gun, such as the permit to carry". HB 1324 (2025-2026, single-subject, 87-76) repeals the silencer definition in O.C.G.A. 16-11-121 and takes silencers off the dangerous-weapons possession ban of 16-11-122, whose only exception (16-11-124) is registration "in accordance with the dictates of the National Firearms Act"; that is removing an accessory from a registration law, rung 5. His other acts are on the loosening side only: Yeas on HB 218 and SB 259 (carry widening, rung 4), HB 1018 and a sponsorship of HB 1454 (registry bans, direction-only); Yea on HB 1023 (2025-2026, weapon detection in school buildings, 151-11) is near-unanimous and gated; it is also a school-safety measure (adjacent) and "weapon" there is wider than firearms. Yea on SB 204 (2025-2026) is on a multi-subject bill whose gun part is state preemption (adjacent), so it shows no rung. No rung 1-3 act, so the furthest act is rung 5.$q$),
  ('82a848e0-1b39-44c5-8b18-d8f4bc25325e', 3,
   $q$He is a sponsor (LegiScan signer) of HB 3 (2025), single-subject and with no floor vote on file, so not gated, whose operative text says 'No unlicensed person shall transfer a firearm unless such transfer is facilitated by a dealer', with the dealer running an NICS check: checking every buyer, including private sales, which is the rung 'Add limits on buying, having or carrying a gun, such as checking every buyer'. On his own site (2025-10-24, in-term) he wrote that 'Passing HB 3 would be the first significant step towards protecting Georgians from gun violence'. His other gun acts are No votes on carry and permit bills (direction only). His Yea on HB 1018 (2024) is a registry ban plus merchant-code rules (R5-L: direction-only and adjacent), so it does not make the record two-sided. HB 1023 is gated (151-11) and SB 204 is multi-subject.$q$,
   $q$He is a sponsor (LegiScan signer) of HB 3 (2025), single-subject and with no floor vote on file, so not gated, whose operative text says 'No unlicensed person shall transfer a firearm unless such transfer is facilitated by a dealer', with the dealer running an NICS check: checking every buyer, including private sales, which is the rung 'Add limits on buying, having or carrying a gun, such as checking every buyer'. On his own site (2025-10-24, in-term) he wrote that 'Passing HB 3 would be the first significant step towards protecting Georgians from gun violence'. His other gun acts are No votes on carry and permit bills (direction only). His Yea on HB 1018 (2024) is a registry ban plus merchant-code rules (direction-only and adjacent), so it does not make the record two-sided. HB 1023 is gated (151-11) and SB 204 is multi-subject.$q$),
  ('831ea2bb-f83f-4228-b1f3-00bf660df88b', 5,
   $q$Rung 5, "Repeal laws that require a permit or registration for a gun, such as the permit to carry": a Yea on HB 1324 (2025-26; House Vote #665, 2026-03-06, 87-76; single-subject, not gated), which strikes "silencer" from O.C.G.A. 16-11-122 ("No person shall have in his or her possession any sawed-off shotgun, sawed-off rifle, machine gun, or dangerous weapon" … "except as provided in Code Section 16-11-124"), a ban whose exception is items registered "in accordance with the dictates of the National Firearms Act": removing an accessory from a registration law (R5-AZ). Her other gun records show no other rung: HB 1023 (weapon detection in school buildings, "weapon" wider than firearms) is a school-safety measure (adjacent) and gated near-unanimous (151-11); SB 204 (2025) is multi-subject and its gun part is preemption (adjacent). No rung 1-3 act is on file.$q$,
   $q$Rung 5, "Repeal laws that require a permit or registration for a gun, such as the permit to carry": a Yea on HB 1324 (2025-26; House Vote #665, 2026-03-06, 87-76; single-subject, not gated), which strikes "silencer" from O.C.G.A. 16-11-122 ("No person shall have in his or her possession any sawed-off shotgun, sawed-off rifle, machine gun, or dangerous weapon" … "except as provided in Code Section 16-11-124"), a ban whose exception is items registered "in accordance with the dictates of the National Firearms Act": removing an accessory from a registration law. Her other gun records show no other rung: HB 1023 (weapon detection in school buildings, "weapon" wider than firearms) is a school-safety measure (adjacent) and gated near-unanimous (151-11); SB 204 (2025) is multi-subject and its gun part is preemption (adjacent). No rung 1-3 act is on file.$q$),
  ('83f36fc7-cc16-4420-a0c0-5025af823bdf', 5,
   $q$Yea on HB 1324 (2026-03-06). HB 1324 (2025-2026, single-subject, 87-76) repeals the silencer definition in O.C.G.A. 16-11-121 and takes silencers off the dangerous-weapons possession ban of 16-11-122, whose only exception (16-11-124) is registration "in accordance with the dictates of the National Firearms Act"; under R5-AZ that is removing an accessory from a registration law, rung 5. His 2024 questionnaire words ("gun free zones are targets") show the loosening side only, his teacher-carry answer is out of scope (one named group carrying for its work), and "STAUNCH DEFENDER OF THE 2ND AMENDMENT" is rhetorical; none contradicts the vote. Yea on HB 1023 (2025-2026, weapon detection in school buildings, 151-11) is near-unanimous and gated; it is also a school-safety measure (adjacent) and "weapon" there is wider than firearms. Yea on SB 204 (2025-2026) is on a multi-subject bill whose gun part is state preemption (H12, adjacent), so it shows no rung. No rung 1-4 act, so he sits on rung 5.$q$,
   $q$Yea on HB 1324 (2026-03-06). HB 1324 (2025-2026, single-subject, 87-76) repeals the silencer definition in O.C.G.A. 16-11-121 and takes silencers off the dangerous-weapons possession ban of 16-11-122, whose only exception (16-11-124) is registration "in accordance with the dictates of the National Firearms Act"; that is removing an accessory from a registration law, rung 5. His 2024 questionnaire words calling gun-free zones targets show the loosening side only, his teacher-carry answer is out of scope (one named group carrying for its work), and his description of himself as a staunch defender of the Second Amendment is rhetorical; none contradicts the vote. Yea on HB 1023 (2025-2026, weapon detection in school buildings, 151-11) is near-unanimous and gated; it is also a school-safety measure (adjacent) and "weapon" there is wider than firearms. Yea on SB 204 (2025-2026) is on a multi-subject bill whose gun part is state preemption (adjacent), so it shows no rung. No rung 1-4 act, so he sits on rung 5.$q$),
  ('86fdf1eb-2200-46fb-b1fd-63f15acef171', 5,
   $q$Sponsor of, and Yea on, GA SB 319 (2021-2022, Act 596) provides that "no person shall carry a weapon unless he or she is a lawful weapons carrier", where a lawful weapons carrier is any person "licensed or eligible for a license", which ends the licence requirement to carry for eligible adults (Senate final vote 2022-04-01, 34-22, counted once). That meets rung 5, "Repeal laws that require a permit or registration for a gun, such as the permit to carry". His other acts (HB 218 reciprocity, which he carried and described as "Anyone with a concealed weapons permit in another state that comes into Georgia will have reciprocity"; SB 259; SB 214 emergency-powers carry; SB 478 park carry) are rung 4, below the furthest act. No rung 1-3 act is on file.$q$,
   $q$Sponsor of, and Yea on, GA SB 319 (2021-2022, Act 596) provides that "no person shall carry a weapon unless he or she is a lawful weapons carrier", where a lawful weapons carrier is any person "licensed or eligible for a license", which ends the licence requirement to carry for eligible adults (Senate final vote 2022-04-01, 34-22, counted once). That meets rung 5, "Repeal laws that require a permit or registration for a gun, such as the permit to carry". His other acts (HB 218 reciprocity, which he carried and described as honouring carry permits from other states; SB 259; SB 214 emergency-powers carry; SB 478 park carry) are rung 4, below the furthest act. No rung 1-3 act is on file.$q$),
  ('87a2e859-15cc-4379-a0b7-60e4e21013df', 5,
   $q$Yea on House passage of SB 319 (2021-22, House Vote #817, 2022-03-30, 100-67; single-subject, ungated), which makes 'Lawful weapons carrier' mean any person 'licensed or eligible for a license' and provides 'no person shall carry a weapon unless he or she is a lawful weapons carrier', so the licence is no longer required to carry: this repeals the permit to carry, rung 5 ('Repeal laws that require a permit or registration for a gun, such as the permit to carry'). Yea on HB 1324 (2025-26, House Vote #665, 2026-03-06, 87-76; single-subject) takes the silencer off the O.C.G.A. 16-11-122 dangerous-weapons ban whose exception (16-11-124(4)) is registration under the National Firearms Act, a second rung 5 act under R5-AZ. SB 319 appears twice in the store (GA-SB-319-2021 and GA-SB-319-2022, the same House vote) and is counted once. His Yeas on HB 218 (carry reciprocity) and SB 259 (carry in places of worship, online permit application) are rung 4 acts below the furthest act; HB 1018 (registry ban, merchant codes) is direction-only/adjacent, SB 204 is multi-subject, HB 1023 (school weapon detection, a school-safety measure) is near-unanimous (151-11) and gated. No rung 1-3 act is on file, so the record is one-sided.$q$,
   $q$Yea on House passage of SB 319 (2021-22, House Vote #817, 2022-03-30, 100-67; single-subject, ungated), which makes 'Lawful weapons carrier' mean any person 'licensed or eligible for a license' and provides 'no person shall carry a weapon unless he or she is a lawful weapons carrier', so the licence is no longer required to carry: this repeals the permit to carry, rung 5 ('Repeal laws that require a permit or registration for a gun, such as the permit to carry'). Yea on HB 1324 (2025-26, House Vote #665, 2026-03-06, 87-76; single-subject) takes the silencer off the O.C.G.A. 16-11-122 dangerous-weapons ban whose exception (16-11-124(4)) is registration under the National Firearms Act, a second rung 5 act. SB 319 appears twice in the store (GA-SB-319-2021 and GA-SB-319-2022, the same House vote) and is counted once. His Yeas on HB 218 (carry reciprocity) and SB 259 (carry in places of worship, online permit application) are rung 4 acts below the furthest act; HB 1018 (registry ban, merchant codes) is direction-only/adjacent, SB 204 is multi-subject, HB 1023 (school weapon detection, a school-safety measure) is near-unanimous (151-11) and gated. No rung 1-3 act is on file, so the record is one-sided.$q$),
  ('88fd133e-60e5-49a9-a7b4-7dcc2659f7b5', 5,
   $q$Furthest act is rung 5. He voted Yea on SB 319 and on HB 1324. GA-SB-319-2021 (single-subject; Georgia Constitutional Carry Act) lets a lawful weapons carrier "have or carry on his or her person a weapon or long gun" … "without a valid weapons carry license": it repeals the requirement of a permit to carry, which is rung 5 ("Repeal laws that require a permit" … "such as the permit to carry"). House passage 2022-03-30, 100-67, census gate none. GA-HB-1324-2025 (single-subject) strikes "or silencer" from O.C.G.A. 16-11-122 ("No person shall have in his or her possession any sawed-off shotgun, sawed-off rifle, machine gun, or dangerous weapon, or silencer") and 16-11-123, a ban whose only exception (16-11-124) is registration "in accordance with the dictates of the National Firearms Act". Under R5-AZ, taking a type of firearm item off a ban whose only exception is federal registration is a rung 5 act. House passage 2026-03-06, 87-76, census gate none. The GA-SB-319-2022 finding (7017959d...) duplicates the same House roll call and has no instrument file, so it is counted once, through GA-SB-319-2021. The person's other gun Yeas (HB 218 reciprocity, SB 259) are rung 4 or loosening direction-only acts; none is a rung 1-3 act, so nothing conflicts with rung 5. GA-HB-1023-2025 (weapon detection in school buildings; "weapon" there is wider than firearms) is read as a school-safety measure, adjacent, and its vote was near-unanimous (151-11), so it is gated either way. GA-SB-204-2025 is multi-subject (criminal charging by accusation plus state preemption), so its vote shows a direction at most (V4.1). GA-HB-1018-2023: under R5-L the merchant-code part is adjacent and the registry bar, where no registry exists, is direction-only (loosening side); not a rung act. Evidence basis: record.$q$,
