   $q$Yea on SB 319 House passage, 2022-03-30. SB 319 (2021-2022, the Constitutional Carry Act; stored twice as GA-SB-319-2021 and GA-SB-319-2022, counted once) is single-subject and passed 100-67 in the House and 34-22 on the Senate agree vote (not gated). Its operative text makes the carrier a "lawful weapons carrier", meaning anyone "licensed or eligible for a license", and provides "no person shall carry a weapon unless he or she is a lawful weapons carrier", so the carry licence is no longer required: rung 5, "Repeal laws that require a permit or registration for a gun, such as the permit to carry". He is also a sponsor of HB 1324 and voted Yea on it (2026, 87-76): silencers struck from the ban whose only exception is NFA registration (rung 5); he is listed on SB 499 (same act) too. Yeas on HB 218, HB 280, HB 292, SB 259 and the HB 1378 sponsorship are rung 4 on the same side; HB 1018 and HB 1454 (registry bars) are direction-only. His 2025 words that training should be optional show a side only. No rung 1-3 act.$q$),
  ('9bf773d5-7272-4fe0-9033-e3bd8930836c', 5,
   $q$He prime-sponsored and voted Yea (Senate final concurrence 2022-04-01, 34-22) on GA SB 319 (2022), whose enacted text says 'no person shall carry a weapon unless he or she is a lawful weapons carrier', a lawful weapons carrier being anyone 'licensed or eligible for a license': it removes the permit to carry for eligible adults, which meets 'Repeal laws that require a permit or registration for a gun, such as the permit to carry'. He also sponsored and voted Yea (2026-03-06, 30-20) on SB 499, which strikes 'silencer' from O.C.G.A. 16-11-122's ban whose only exception is NFA registration (R5-AZ, rung 5). His other acts (HB 218 reciprocity, SB 259 worship carry and online permits) are rung 4, HB 1018 is direction-only, SB 67 is closure C2 and SB 204 is multi-subject; none is a rung 1-3 act, so the furthest act is rung 5. SB 319 appears twice in the store (GA-SB-319-2022 and GA-SB-319-2021); counted once.$q$,
   $q$He prime-sponsored and voted Yea (Senate final concurrence 2022-04-01, 34-22) on GA SB 319 (2022), whose enacted text says 'no person shall carry a weapon unless he or she is a lawful weapons carrier', a lawful weapons carrier being anyone 'licensed or eligible for a license': it removes the permit to carry for eligible adults, which meets 'Repeal laws that require a permit or registration for a gun, such as the permit to carry'. He also sponsored and voted Yea (2026-03-06, 30-20) on SB 499, which strikes 'silencer' from O.C.G.A. 16-11-122's ban whose only exception is NFA registration (rung 5). His other acts (HB 218 reciprocity, SB 259 worship carry and online permits) are rung 4, HB 1018 is direction-only, SB 67 (a bar on enforcing federal gun rules) is adjacent and SB 204 is multi-subject; none is a rung 1-3 act, so the furthest act is rung 5. SB 319 appears twice in the store (GA-SB-319-2022 and GA-SB-319-2021); counted once.$q$),
  ('9cdee7d8-43d6-4596-8982-55b006f7754c', 3,
   $q$She is a listed sponsor of HB 3 (2025) and HB 44 (2023), which say "No unlicensed person shall transfer a firearm unless such transfer is facilitated by a dealer" with a background check, and SB 179 (2021, from her Senate term, same legislature) to the same effect; HB 4 (2025) and HB 45 (2023), a three-day waiting period; HB 584 (2023), a red-flag order; and SB 229 (2021), repealing campus carry. None had a floor vote. They meet rung 3, "Add limits on buying, having or carrying a gun, such as checking every buyer, red-flag orders, waiting periods". HB 64 (insurance) and HB 135/HB 161 (storage) are out of scope; her Yea on HB 1018 is the registry-ban/merchant-code bill (direction-only and adjacent under R5-L), which does not make the record two-sided; she has no rung 4 or 5 act.$q$,
   $q$She is a listed sponsor of HB 3 (2025) and HB 44 (2023), which say "No unlicensed person shall transfer a firearm unless such transfer is facilitated by a dealer" with a background check, and SB 179 (2021, from her Senate term, same legislature) to the same effect; HB 4 (2025) and HB 45 (2023), a three-day waiting period; HB 584 (2023), a red-flag order; and SB 229 (2021), repealing campus carry. None had a floor vote. They meet rung 3, "Add limits on buying, having or carrying a gun, such as checking every buyer, red-flag orders, waiting periods". HB 64 (insurance) and HB 135/HB 161 (storage) are out of scope; her Yea on HB 1018 is the registry-ban/merchant-code bill (direction-only and adjacent), which does not make the record two-sided; she has no rung 4 or 5 act.$q$),
  ('9db68acc-1e60-4bac-bb16-2738aee1a087', 5,
   $q$Rung 5 (repeal laws that require a permit or registration for a gun, such as the permit to carry). He voted Yea on GA SB 319 in the House (2022-03-30, 100-67), whose enacted text says 'no person shall carry a weapon unless he or she is a lawful weapons carrier' (anyone 'licensed or eligible for a license'), ending the carry-permit requirement, and Yea on HB 1324 (2026-03-06, 87-76), which strikes 'silencer' from the O.C.G.A. 16-11-122 ban whose only exception is NFA registration (R5-AZ: rung 5). His other votes (HB 218, HB 280, HB 292, HB 859, HB 1060, SB 259, HB 1018) are on the loosening side; no rung 1-3 act. HB 1023 (151-11) is gated and SB 204 is multi-subject.$q$,
   $q$Rung 5 (repeal laws that require a permit or registration for a gun, such as the permit to carry). He voted Yea on GA SB 319 in the House (2022-03-30, 100-67), whose enacted text says 'no person shall carry a weapon unless he or she is a lawful weapons carrier' (anyone 'licensed or eligible for a license'), ending the carry-permit requirement, and Yea on HB 1324 (2026-03-06, 87-76), which strikes 'silencer' from the O.C.G.A. 16-11-122 ban whose only exception is NFA registration (rung 5). His other votes (HB 218, HB 280, HB 292, HB 859, HB 1060, SB 259, HB 1018) are on the loosening side; no rung 1-3 act. HB 1023 (151-11) is gated and SB 204 is multi-subject.$q$),
  ('9e89b166-cbb1-48f2-94e9-986171491880', 5,
   $q$Yea on the House passage of SB 319 (2022-03-30, counted once) and Yea on HB 1324 (2026-03-06). SB 319 (2021-2022, single-subject, 100-67 House / 34-22 Senate) makes it so "no person shall carry a weapon unless he or she is a lawful weapons carrier", defined as "any person who is licensed or eligible for a license", which removes the permit to carry for eligible adults: "Repeal laws that require a permit or registration for a gun, such as the permit to carry". HB 1324 (2025-2026, single-subject, 87-76) repeals the silencer definition in O.C.G.A. 16-11-121 and takes silencers off the dangerous-weapons possession ban of 16-11-122, whose only exception (16-11-124) is registration "in accordance with the dictates of the National Firearms Act"; under R5-AZ that is removing an accessory from a registration law, rung 5. His other acts are loosening: Yeas on HB 859 and HB 1060 (2016), HB 280 and HB 292 (2017), HB 218 and SB 259 (2022) (rung 4) and HB 1018 (direction-only). Yea on HB 1023 (2025-2026, weapon detection in school buildings, 151-11) is near-unanimous and gated; it is also a school-safety measure (adjacent) and "weapon" there is wider than firearms. Yea on SB 204 (2025-2026) is on a multi-subject bill whose gun part is state preemption (H12, adjacent), so it shows no rung. No rung 1-3 act, so the furthest act is rung 5.$q$,
   $q$Yea on the House passage of SB 319 (2022-03-30, counted once) and Yea on HB 1324 (2026-03-06). SB 319 (2021-2022, single-subject, 100-67 House / 34-22 Senate) makes it so "no person shall carry a weapon unless he or she is a lawful weapons carrier", defined as "any person who is licensed or eligible for a license", which removes the permit to carry for eligible adults: "Repeal laws that require a permit or registration for a gun, such as the permit to carry". HB 1324 (2025-2026, single-subject, 87-76) repeals the silencer definition in O.C.G.A. 16-11-121 and takes silencers off the dangerous-weapons possession ban of 16-11-122, whose only exception (16-11-124) is registration "in accordance with the dictates of the National Firearms Act"; that is removing an accessory from a registration law, rung 5. His other acts are loosening: Yeas on HB 859 and HB 1060 (2016), HB 280 and HB 292 (2017), HB 218 and SB 259 (2022) (rung 4) and HB 1018 (direction-only). Yea on HB 1023 (2025-2026, weapon detection in school buildings, 151-11) is near-unanimous and gated; it is also a school-safety measure (adjacent) and "weapon" there is wider than firearms. Yea on SB 204 (2025-2026) is on a multi-subject bill whose gun part is state preemption (adjacent), so it shows no rung. No rung 1-3 act, so the furthest act is rung 5.$q$),
  ('a1c5fa39-f958-443c-b33c-55fabb955886', 5,
   $q$Yea on SB 319 (2021-22), House passage 2022-03-30, 100-67 (one roll call, filed under both GA-SB-319-2021 and GA-SB-319-2022; counted once): the enacted text says "no person shall carry a weapon unless he or she is a lawful weapons carrier" and defines that as "any person who is licensed or eligible for a license", so the licence to carry a handgun is no longer required; and Yea on HB 1324 (2025-26), House passage 2026-03-06, 87-76: it repeals the silencer definition in O.C.G.A. 16-11-121 and strikes "silencer" from the 16-11-122 possession ban, whose exception is items registered "in accordance with the dictates of the National Firearms Act"; under R5-AZ that is removing an accessory from a registration law. Both meet rung 5, "Repeal laws that require a permit or registration for a gun, such as the permit to carry". His other acts (HB 218, SB 259) are rung 4 and HB 1018 is direction-only; he has no rung 1-3 act. His campaign line about "enhancing our 2nd Amendment right" names no rule and is not relied on.$q$,
   $q$Yea on SB 319 (2021-22), House passage 2022-03-30, 100-67 (one roll call, filed under both GA-SB-319-2021 and GA-SB-319-2022; counted once): the enacted text says "no person shall carry a weapon unless he or she is a lawful weapons carrier" and defines that as "any person who is licensed or eligible for a license", so the licence to carry a handgun is no longer required; and Yea on HB 1324 (2025-26), House passage 2026-03-06, 87-76: it repeals the silencer definition in O.C.G.A. 16-11-121 and strikes "silencer" from the 16-11-122 possession ban, whose exception is items registered "in accordance with the dictates of the National Firearms Act"; that is removing an accessory from a registration law. Both meet rung 5, "Repeal laws that require a permit or registration for a gun, such as the permit to carry". His other acts (HB 218, SB 259) are rung 4 and HB 1018 is direction-only; he has no rung 1-3 act. His campaign line about strengthening Second Amendment rights names no rule and is not relied on.$q$),
  ('a386c7f4-6801-4103-8755-61032e6b85e3', 5,
   $q$Furthest act is rung 5. He voted Yea on SB 319, voted Yea on HB 1324, and is a listed sponsor of HB 1324 (LegiScan sponsor list; a Georgia listed signer counts as a sponsor). GA-SB-319-2021 (single-subject; Georgia Constitutional Carry Act) lets a lawful weapons carrier "have or carry on his or her person a weapon or long gun" … "without a valid weapons carry license": it repeals the requirement of a permit to carry, which is rung 5 ("Repeal laws that require a permit" … "such as the permit to carry"). House passage 2022-03-30, 100-67, census gate none. GA-HB-1324-2025 (single-subject) strikes "or silencer" from O.C.G.A. 16-11-122 ("No person shall have in his or her possession any sawed-off shotgun, sawed-off rifle, machine gun, or dangerous weapon, or silencer") and 16-11-123, a ban whose only exception (16-11-124) is registration "in accordance with the dictates of the National Firearms Act". Under R5-AZ, taking a type of firearm item off a ban whose only exception is federal registration is a rung 5 act. House passage 2026-03-06, 87-76, census gate none. The GA-SB-319-2022 finding (7017959d...) duplicates the same House roll call and has no instrument file, so it is counted once, through GA-SB-319-2021. The person's other gun Yeas (HB 218 reciprocity, HB 280/HB 859 campus carry for licence holders, SB 259, HB 292, HB 1060) are rung 4 or loosening direction-only acts; none is a rung 1-3 act, so nothing conflicts with rung 5. His HB 1454 sponsorship (registry bar, discharge limits, sale of police guns) is loosening direction-only. GA-HB-1023-2025 (weapon detection in school buildings; "weapon" there is wider than firearms) is read as a school-safety measure, adjacent, and its vote was near-unanimous (151-11), so it is gated either way. GA-SB-204-2025 is multi-subject (criminal charging by accusation plus state preemption), so its vote shows a direction at most (V4.1). GA-HB-1018-2023: under R5-L the merchant-code part is adjacent and the registry bar, where no registry exists, is direction-only (loosening side); not a rung act. Evidence basis: record.$q$,
