   $q$Rung 5, "Repeal laws that require a permit or registration for a gun, such as the permit to carry": a Yea on SB 319 (2021-22; House Vote #817, 2022-03-30, 100-67; single-subject, not gated), whose operative text defines a "lawful weapons carrier" as "any person who is licensed or eligible for a license" and provides "no person shall carry a weapon unless he or she is a lawful weapons carrier", so a carry licence is no longer required (the store holds this vote twice, as GA-SB-319-2021 and GA-SB-319-2022; it is counted once), and a Yea on HB 1324 (2025-26; House Vote #665, 2026-03-06, 87-76; single-subject, not gated), which strikes "silencer" from O.C.G.A. 16-11-122 ("No person shall have in his or her possession any sawed-off shotgun, sawed-off rifle, machine gun, or dangerous weapon" … "except as provided in Code Section 16-11-124"), a ban whose exception is items registered "in accordance with the dictates of the National Firearms Act": removing an accessory from a registration law (R5-AZ). Yeas on rung 4 carry bills (HB 218, SB 259, HB 280, HB 859, HB 292, HB 1060) are on the same side and sit under the furthest act. HB 1018 (2023-24) is a merchant-code rule (adjacent) plus a bar on a gun-owner registry where none exists (direction-only, R5-L); HB 1023 (weapon detection at school entrances, "weapon" wider than firearms) is a school-safety measure (adjacent) and is gated near-unanimous (151-11); SB 204 (2025) is multi-subject and its gun part is preemption (adjacent). No rung 1-3 act is on file. His sponsorship of HB 1023 is gated like its 151-11 vote; his 2025 quote on SB 204 ("protected from government overreach") is about a preemption bill and names no rung (rhetorical).$q$,
