  ('b1e28dc7-f861-4912-b37b-5398f8bed0fa', 5,
   $q$Yea on GA SB 319 (2021-2022, Act 596) provides that "no person shall carry a weapon unless he or she is a lawful weapons carrier", where a lawful weapons carrier is any person "licensed or eligible for a license", which ends the licence requirement to carry for eligible adults (House, 2022-03-30, 100-67), and Yea on GA HB 1324 (2025-2026) takes silencers off Georgia's dangerous-weapons ban (O.C.G.A. 16-11-122, "No person shall have in his or her possession any sawed-off shotgun, sawed-off rifle, machine gun, or dangerous weapon, or silencer except as provided in Code Section 16-11-124", with "or silencer" struck and the silencer definition in 16-11-121(7) repealed), a ban whose exception is possession "registered" … "in accordance with the dictates of the National Firearms Act"; under R5-AZ that is removing an accessory from a registration law (2026-03-06, 87-76); both meet rung 5, "Repeal laws that require a permit or registration for a gun, such as the permit to carry". Her 2020 questionnaire answer (the campaign that seated her), "I support the 2nd Amendment and Constitutional Carry", agrees. Her HB 218 and SB 259 Yeas are rung 4, below the furthest act; her teacher-carry Yes is out of scope. No rung 1-3 act is on file.$q$,
   $q$Yea on GA SB 319 (2021-2022, Act 596) provides that "no person shall carry a weapon unless he or she is a lawful weapons carrier", where a lawful weapons carrier is any person "licensed or eligible for a license", which ends the licence requirement to carry for eligible adults (House, 2022-03-30, 100-67), and Yea on GA HB 1324 (2025-2026) takes silencers off Georgia's dangerous-weapons ban (O.C.G.A. 16-11-122, "No person shall have in his or her possession any sawed-off shotgun, sawed-off rifle, machine gun, or dangerous weapon, or silencer except as provided in Code Section 16-11-124", with "or silencer" struck and the silencer definition in 16-11-121(7) repealed), a ban whose exception is possession "registered" … "in accordance with the dictates of the National Firearms Act"; that is removing an accessory from a registration law (2026-03-06, 87-76); both meet rung 5, "Repeal laws that require a permit or registration for a gun, such as the permit to carry". Her 2020 questionnaire answer (the campaign that seated her), "I support the 2nd Amendment and Constitutional Carry", agrees. Her HB 218 and SB 259 Yeas are rung 4, below the furthest act; her teacher-carry Yes is out of scope. No rung 1-3 act is on file.$q$),
  ('b2cf2867-f38d-4064-86f2-49786c04730f', 5,
   $q$Yea on HB 1324 (2026-03-06). HB 1324 (2025-2026, single-subject, 87-76) repeals the silencer definition in O.C.G.A. 16-11-121 and takes silencers off the dangerous-weapons possession ban of 16-11-122, whose only exception (16-11-124) is registration "in accordance with the dictates of the National Firearms Act"; under R5-AZ that is removing an accessory from a registration law, rung 5. His Yea on HB 1018 is direction-only, loosening side (R5-L); Yea on HB 1023 (2025-2026, weapon detection in school buildings, 151-11) is near-unanimous and gated; it is also a school-safety measure (adjacent) and "weapon" there is wider than firearms. Yea on SB 204 (2025-2026) is on a multi-subject bill whose gun part is state preemption (H12, adjacent), so it shows no rung. No rung 1-4 act, so he sits on rung 5.$q$,
   $q$Yea on HB 1324 (2026-03-06). HB 1324 (2025-2026, single-subject, 87-76) repeals the silencer definition in O.C.G.A. 16-11-121 and takes silencers off the dangerous-weapons possession ban of 16-11-122, whose only exception (16-11-124) is registration "in accordance with the dictates of the National Firearms Act"; that is removing an accessory from a registration law, rung 5. His Yea on HB 1018 is direction-only, loosening side (its bar on a gun-owner registry where none exists repeals nothing); Yea on HB 1023 (2025-2026, weapon detection in school buildings, 151-11) is near-unanimous and gated; it is also a school-safety measure (adjacent) and "weapon" there is wider than firearms. Yea on SB 204 (2025-2026) is on a multi-subject bill whose gun part is state preemption (adjacent), so it shows no rung. No rung 1-4 act, so he sits on rung 5.$q$),
  ('b3519ced-87eb-4e21-ac0e-803355eeac1c', 5,
   $q$Furthest act is rung 5. She voted Yea on SB 319 and on HB 1324. GA-SB-319-2021 (single-subject; Georgia Constitutional Carry Act) lets a lawful weapons carrier "have or carry on his or her person a weapon or long gun" … "without a valid weapons carry license": it repeals the requirement of a permit to carry, which is rung 5 ("Repeal laws that require a permit" … "such as the permit to carry"). House passage 2022-03-30, 100-67, census gate none. GA-HB-1324-2025 (single-subject) strikes "or silencer" from O.C.G.A. 16-11-122 ("No person shall have in his or her possession any sawed-off shotgun, sawed-off rifle, machine gun, or dangerous weapon, or silencer") and 16-11-123, a ban whose only exception (16-11-124) is registration "in accordance with the dictates of the National Firearms Act". Under R5-AZ, taking a type of firearm item off a ban whose only exception is federal registration is a rung 5 act. House passage 2026-03-06, 87-76, census gate none. The GA-SB-319-2022 finding (7017959d...) duplicates the same House roll call and has no instrument file, so it is counted once, through GA-SB-319-2021. The person's other gun Yeas (HB 218 reciprocity, HB 280/HB 859 campus carry for licence holders, SB 259, HB 292, HB 1060) are rung 4 or loosening direction-only acts; none is a rung 1-3 act, so nothing conflicts with rung 5. Her 2010 words on SB 291 ("expands where licensed holders may lawfully carry" … "and eases their license renewal process") describe a rung 4 act and are not relied on. GA-HB-1023-2025 (weapon detection in school buildings; "weapon" there is wider than firearms) is read as a school-safety measure, adjacent, and its vote was near-unanimous (151-11), so it is gated either way. GA-SB-204-2025 is multi-subject (criminal charging by accusation plus state preemption), so its vote shows a direction at most (V4.1). GA-HB-1018-2023: under R5-L the merchant-code part is adjacent and the registry bar, where no registry exists, is direction-only (loosening side); not a rung act. Evidence basis: record.$q$,
   $q$Furthest act is rung 5. She voted Yea on SB 319 and on HB 1324. GA-SB-319-2021 (single-subject; Georgia Constitutional Carry Act) lets a lawful weapons carrier "have or carry on his or her person a weapon or long gun" … "without a valid weapons carry license": it repeals the requirement of a permit to carry, which is rung 5 ("Repeal laws that require a permit" … "such as the permit to carry"). House passage 2022-03-30, 100-67, census gate none. GA-HB-1324-2025 (single-subject) strikes "or silencer" from O.C.G.A. 16-11-122 ("No person shall have in his or her possession any sawed-off shotgun, sawed-off rifle, machine gun, or dangerous weapon, or silencer") and 16-11-123, a ban whose only exception (16-11-124) is registration "in accordance with the dictates of the National Firearms Act". Taking a type of firearm item off a ban whose only exception is federal registration is a rung 5 act. House passage 2026-03-06, 87-76, census gate none. The GA-SB-319-2022 finding (7017959d...) duplicates the same House roll call and has no instrument file, so it is counted once, through GA-SB-319-2021. The person's other gun Yeas (HB 218 reciprocity, HB 280/HB 859 campus carry for licence holders, SB 259, HB 292, HB 1060) are rung 4 or loosening direction-only acts; none is a rung 1-3 act, so nothing conflicts with rung 5. Her 2010 words on SB 291, that it widens where licence holders may carry and eases licence renewal, describe a rung 4 act and are not relied on. GA-HB-1023-2025 (weapon detection in school buildings; "weapon" there is wider than firearms) is read as a school-safety measure, adjacent, and its vote was near-unanimous (151-11), so it is gated either way. GA-SB-204-2025 is multi-subject (criminal charging by accusation plus state preemption), so its vote shows a direction at most (V4.1). GA-HB-1018-2023: the merchant-code part is adjacent and the registry bar, where no registry exists, is direction-only (loosening side); not a rung act. Evidence basis: record.$q$),
  ('b8cc3d66-9849-4586-acf1-b933bc920036', 5,
   $q$Rung 5, "Repeal laws that require a permit or registration for a gun, such as the permit to carry": prime sponsor of SB 319 and a Yea on SB 319 (2021-22; Senate Vote #783, agree to House substitute, 2022-04-01, 34-22; single-subject, not gated), whose operative text defines a "lawful weapons carrier" as "any person who is licensed or eligible for a license" and provides "no person shall carry a weapon unless he or she is a lawful weapons carrier", so a carry licence is no longer required (the same final vote appears twice in the store and is counted once; the earlier third-reading vote is not the record); and SB 499 (2025-26; sponsor, and Yea on Senate Vote #686, 2026-03-06, 30-20; single-subject, not gated), which strikes "silencer" from the O.C.G.A. 16-11-122 possession ban whose exception is National Firearms Act registration, so that "The exemption under this paragraph does not include the possession of silencers" (R5-AZ: removing an accessory from a registration law). His 2022 words on the permit cost during the SB 319 debate show the same side. Other acts: Yeas on HB 218, SB 259, HB 280, HB 292 are rung 4 acts under the furthest act; sponsorship of SB 67 is adjacent (closure C2); HB 1018 is adjacent plus direction-only (R5-L); SB 204 is multi-subject preemption (adjacent). No rung 1-3 act is on file.$q$,
   $q$Rung 5, "Repeal laws that require a permit or registration for a gun, such as the permit to carry": prime sponsor of SB 319 and a Yea on SB 319 (2021-22; Senate Vote #783, agree to House substitute, 2022-04-01, 34-22; single-subject, not gated), whose operative text defines a "lawful weapons carrier" as "any person who is licensed or eligible for a license" and provides "no person shall carry a weapon unless he or she is a lawful weapons carrier", so a carry licence is no longer required (the same final vote appears twice in the store and is counted once; the earlier third-reading vote is not the record); and SB 499 (2025-26; sponsor, and Yea on Senate Vote #686, 2026-03-06, 30-20; single-subject, not gated), which strikes "silencer" from the O.C.G.A. 16-11-122 possession ban whose exception is National Firearms Act registration, so that "The exemption under this paragraph does not include the possession of silencers" (removing an accessory from a registration law). His 2022 words on the permit cost during the SB 319 debate show the same side. Other acts: Yeas on HB 218, SB 259, HB 280, HB 292 are rung 4 acts under the furthest act; sponsorship of SB 67 is adjacent; HB 1018 is adjacent plus direction-only (a merchant-code rule, plus a bar on a gun-owner registry where none exists); SB 204 is multi-subject preemption (adjacent). No rung 1-3 act is on file.$q$),
