   $q$Yea on SB 319 House passage, 2022-03-30. SB 319 (2021-2022, the Constitutional Carry Act; stored twice as GA-SB-319-2021 and GA-SB-319-2022, counted once) is single-subject and passed 100-67 in the House and 34-22 on the Senate agree vote (not gated). Its operative text makes the carrier a "lawful weapons carrier", meaning anyone "licensed or eligible for a license", and provides "no person shall carry a weapon unless he or she is a lawful weapons carrier", so the carry licence is no longer required: rung 5, "Repeal laws that require a permit or registration for a gun, such as the permit to carry". Yea on HB 1324 (2026) is a second rung 5 act: silencers struck from the ban whose only exception is NFA registration. His Yeas on HB 218, HB 280, HB 859, HB 292, HB 1060 and SB 259 (carry reciprocity and campus or church carry) are rung 4 acts on the same side; HB 1018 is direction-only (its bar on a gun-owner registry where none exists repeals nothing). No rung 1-3 act is on his record, so rung 5 is the furthest act.$q$),
  ('deaded26-7697-4b7b-b538-61f2843b139d', 5,
   $q$Yea on HB 1324 (2025-26), House passage 2026-03-06, 87-76: it repeals the silencer definition in O.C.G.A. 16-11-121 and strikes "silencer" from the 16-11-122 possession ban, whose exception is items registered "in accordance with the dictates of the National Firearms Act"; under R5-AZ that is removing an accessory from a registration law. That meets rung 5, "Repeal laws that require a permit or registration for a gun". His Yea on HB 1018 (registry ban) is direction-only, HB 1023 adjacent and gated, SB 204 multi-subject; he has no rung 1-3 act.$q$,
   $q$Yea on HB 1324 (2025-26), House passage 2026-03-06, 87-76: it repeals the silencer definition in O.C.G.A. 16-11-121 and strikes "silencer" from the 16-11-122 possession ban, whose exception is items registered "in accordance with the dictates of the National Firearms Act"; that is removing an accessory from a registration law. That meets rung 5, "Repeal laws that require a permit or registration for a gun". His Yea on HB 1018 (registry ban) is direction-only, HB 1023 adjacent and gated, SB 204 multi-subject; he has no rung 1-3 act.$q$),
  ('e55a8fb1-8fd5-4fa0-8254-eaf9422c127e', 5,
   $q$He cast a Yea on SB 319 (2022 House Vote #817, 100-67; the same roll call is filed twice in the store and is counted once), whose operative text says 'no person shall carry a weapon unless he or she is a lawful weapons carrier' and defines a lawful weapons carrier as anyone 'licensed or eligible for a license', so the permit to carry is no longer required. That is 'Repeal laws that require a permit or registration for a gun, such as the permit to carry'. He also cast a Yea on HB 1324 (2026 House Vote #665, 87-76), which strikes 'or silencer' from O.C.G.A. 16-11-122's dangerous-weapons ban, whose exception (16-11-124) is NFA registration, i.e. it takes an accessory off a registration law (annex R5-AZ). His Yeas on HB 218 and SB 259 and his sponsorship of HB 560 (carry in places of worship) are rung 4 acts on the same side, so the furthest act (rung 5) governs. HB 1018 is direction-only (R5-L), HB 1023 is gated, and SB 204 is multi-subject. His 2020 words raising questions about permitless carry state no rung and come before these votes.$q$,
   $q$He cast a Yea on SB 319 (2022 House Vote #817, 100-67; the same roll call is filed twice in the store and is counted once), whose operative text says 'no person shall carry a weapon unless he or she is a lawful weapons carrier' and defines a lawful weapons carrier as anyone 'licensed or eligible for a license', so the permit to carry is no longer required. That is 'Repeal laws that require a permit or registration for a gun, such as the permit to carry'. He also cast a Yea on HB 1324 (2026 House Vote #665, 87-76), which strikes 'or silencer' from O.C.G.A. 16-11-122's dangerous-weapons ban, whose exception (16-11-124) is NFA registration, i.e. it takes an accessory off a registration law. His Yeas on HB 218 and SB 259 and his sponsorship of HB 560 (carry in places of worship) are rung 4 acts on the same side, so the furthest act (rung 5) governs. HB 1018 is direction-only (its bar on a gun-owner registry where none exists repeals nothing), HB 1023 is gated, and SB 204 is multi-subject. His 2020 words raising questions about permitless carry state no rung and come before these votes.$q$),
  ('e89092d5-fa51-4bc6-9380-6879dabdf6cf', 5,
   $q$Rung 5, "Repeal laws that require a permit or registration for a gun, such as the permit to carry": a Yea on SB 319 (2021-22; House Vote #817, 2022-03-30, 100-67; single-subject, not gated), whose operative text defines a "lawful weapons carrier" as "any person who is licensed or eligible for a license" and provides "no person shall carry a weapon unless he or she is a lawful weapons carrier", so a carry licence is no longer required (the store holds this vote twice, as GA-SB-319-2021 and GA-SB-319-2022; it is counted once), and a Yea on HB 1324 (2025-26; House Vote #665, 2026-03-06, 87-76; single-subject, not gated), which strikes "silencer" from O.C.G.A. 16-11-122 ("No person shall have in his or her possession any sawed-off shotgun, sawed-off rifle, machine gun, or dangerous weapon" … "except as provided in Code Section 16-11-124"), a ban whose exception is items registered "in accordance with the dictates of the National Firearms Act": removing an accessory from a registration law (R5-AZ). Its other carry votes on the same side (rung 4 acts that keep the permit, e.g. HB 218 reciprocity, SB 259 removing places of worship from the barred list) sit under the furthest act; HB 1018 (2023-24) is a merchant-code rule (adjacent) plus a bar on a gun-owner registry where none exists (direction-only, R5-L); HB 1023 (weapon detection at school entrances, "weapon" wider than firearms) is a school-safety measure (adjacent) and is gated near-unanimous (151-11); SB 204 (2025) is multi-subject and its gun part is preemption (adjacent). No rung 1-3 act is on file. His sponsorship of HB 1023 is gated like its 151-11 vote (and adjacent); his undated campaign line "I support the Second Amendment and the right to bear arms" names no policy (rhetorical).$q$,
   $q$Rung 5, "Repeal laws that require a permit or registration for a gun, such as the permit to carry": a Yea on SB 319 (2021-22; House Vote #817, 2022-03-30, 100-67; single-subject, not gated), whose operative text defines a "lawful weapons carrier" as "any person who is licensed or eligible for a license" and provides "no person shall carry a weapon unless he or she is a lawful weapons carrier", so a carry licence is no longer required (the store holds this vote twice, as GA-SB-319-2021 and GA-SB-319-2022; it is counted once), and a Yea on HB 1324 (2025-26; House Vote #665, 2026-03-06, 87-76; single-subject, not gated), which strikes "silencer" from O.C.G.A. 16-11-122 ("No person shall have in his or her possession any sawed-off shotgun, sawed-off rifle, machine gun, or dangerous weapon" … "except as provided in Code Section 16-11-124"), a ban whose exception is items registered "in accordance with the dictates of the National Firearms Act": removing an accessory from a registration law. Its other carry votes on the same side (rung 4 acts that keep the permit, e.g. HB 218 reciprocity, SB 259 removing places of worship from the barred list) sit under the furthest act; HB 1018 (2023-24) is a merchant-code rule (adjacent) plus a bar on a gun-owner registry where none exists (direction-only); HB 1023 (weapon detection at school entrances, "weapon" wider than firearms) is a school-safety measure (adjacent) and is gated near-unanimous (151-11); SB 204 (2025) is multi-subject and its gun part is preemption (adjacent). No rung 1-3 act is on file. His sponsorship of HB 1023 is gated like its 151-11 vote (and adjacent); his undated campaign line backing the Second Amendment and the right to bear arms names no policy (rhetorical).$q$),
  ('e92714ed-1596-484b-b53a-7d5d37415787', 5,
   $q$Sponsor of, and Yea on, GA SB 319 (2021-2022, Act 596) provides that "no person shall carry a weapon unless he or she is a lawful weapons carrier", where a lawful weapons carrier is any person "licensed or eligible for a license", which ends the licence requirement to carry for eligible adults (Senate final vote 2022-04-01, 34-22, counted once), and sponsor of, and Yea on, GA SB 499 (2025-2026) repeals the silencer definition in O.C.G.A. 16-11-121(7) and strikes "or silencer" from 16-11-122's dangerous-weapons ban, whose exception (16-11-124) is NFA registration; under R5-AZ that is removing an accessory from a registration law (Senate, 2026-03-06, 30-20). Both meet rung 5, "Repeal laws that require a permit or registration for a gun". His HB 218 and SB 259 Yeas are rung 4, below the furthest act. His 2020 campaign words that buyers should "go through the licensing procedure that is set up" are a statement older than, and contradicted by, his 2022 and 2026 records; the record governs. No rung 1-3 act is on file.$q$,
